window.C4_SITE_PAGES = [{"name": "ARC-IMPROVEMENTS.md", "url": "/astron/xyz/ARC-IMPROVEMENTS.md", "text": "# ARC reasoning: what was measured, what was changed, and what it bought\n\nThis document covers one pass over the ASTRA engine aimed at a single target:\nsolving more previously unseen ARC tasks. Nothing here is tuned against an\nanswer key, and no task-specific special case was added. Every number was\nproduced by the code in this directory on the 550 public task files in\n`data/arc/`.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The measurement that decided everything\n\nThe starting engine solves **175/400 ARC-AGI-1 (43.8%)** and **45/150\nARC-AGI-2 (30.0%)** at a 20-second per-task budget. Of its 330 failures, 241\nended with **no fitted hypothesis at all** — not a wrong answer, but nothing\nproposed that even reproduced the demonstrations.\n\nBefore changing anything, three experiments were run to find out *why*.\n\n**Experiment 1 — is it starved of time?** The development split was re-run at a\n60-second budget instead of 20.\n\n| budget | dev solved |\n|---|---:|\n| 20 s | 86 / 236 |\n| 60 s | 87 / 236 |\n\nTripling the compute bought **one task**.\n\n**Experiment 2 — is the ranking losing answers it already has?**\n`bench/diagnose.py` re-runs each task collecting *every* distinct prediction any\nfitted hypothesis produced, and asks where the true answer sits in that list.\n\n| class | dev tasks |\n|---|---:|\n| solved | 100 |\n| right answer at rank 2 | 6 |\n| right answer present but ranked below 2 (**mis-ranked**) | **1** |\n| something fit the demonstrations, but nothing produced the answer | 29 |\n| nothing fit the demonstrations at all | 100 |\n\n**Experiment 3 — is the synthesiser's search too shallow or too narrow?**\n`bench/beam_probe.py` re-runs the general enumerator on 40 unsolved tasks at\nfour settings.\n\n| setting | depth | beam | budget | tasks with any fitting program | correct |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| default | 4 | 1500 | 12 s | 1 / 40 | 0 |\n| deep | 6 | 1500 | 40 s | 1 / 40 | 0 |\n| wide | 4 | 6000 | 40 s | 1 / 40 | 0 |\n| wide + deep | 6 | 6000 | 60 s | 1 / 40 | 0 |\n\nIdentical at every setting.\n\nThe three results agree: **the engine is not short of time, not short of search\ndepth, not short of beam width, and its ranking is very nearly perfect.** What\nit lacks is *expressive reach* — the correct transformation is not in the\nprogram space at any depth. Every change below follows from that, and the work\nthat a different diagnosis would have justified (better scoring, deeper search,\na bigger budget) was deliberately not done, because the measurements say it is\nworth about one task.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What was added\n\n### 2.1 Objects become first-class in the general synthesiser\n\n`engine/objops.py` (new)\n\nThe synthesiser composed grid-to-grid functions — rotate, crop, tile, shift,\nfill — and had no way to say *which thing* to act on. Object operators close\nthat: each is a **selector** crossed with an **action**.\n\n| selectors | largest, smallest, widest box, unique colour, majority colour, unique shape, majority shape, unique size, holed, solid, border-touching, enclosed, square, rectangular, symmetric, multi-coloured, all |\n| --- | --- |\n| **actions** | delete, keep only, crop to, crop as patch, recolour, fill holes, fill box, outline, slide until blocked (4), snap to edge (4) |\n\nEvery operator is an ordinary grid-to-grid function, so it drops into the\nexisting search — and therefore into *every* family built on that search: the\ndepth-4 enumerator, the shallow `compose` pass, the forward `cascade` and the\nbackward `refine`. The composition comes free; only the vocabulary is new.\n\nThey are installed as **seed operators**, applied once to the raw input where\nthe frontier is a single grid, after which the cheap pixel library composes on\ntop. Segmentation is memoised per grid so three hundred operators share one\nconnected-components pass; a full pass over a task's grids costs ~23 ms.\n\n### 2.2 Rows and columns become objects\n\n`engine/objects.py` (extended)\n\nTwo segmentations were added, `rows` and `cols`: every row (or column) of the\ngrid as one object, background included. A band of the grid is a thing rules\ntalk about — \"rows that are all one colour become red\" — and no connectivity\nsegmentation can name it. Every object-level solver inherits them.\n\n### 2.3 Per-object process induction\n\n`engine/solvers/objproc.py` (new)\n\n`objects_map` learns \"which colour does each object become\". That is one action\nfamily out of many. `objproc` keeps its discipline and widens the space:\n\n1. segment under several (segmentation, background) readings;\n2. for every object, **propose** actions and keep only those whose local effect\n   is consistent with the demonstrated output;\n3. find a low-capacity decision function from object features to action that is\n   consistent with every object of every training pair *simultaneously*;\n4. apply the whole labelling and require an exact match on all pairs.\n\nThe action algebra: keep, erase, recolour, bounding-box paints, halo, hole fill,\ntranslate, slide-until-blocked, snap-to-edge, in-place dihedral, ray casting,\n**duplicate at an offset**, **mirrored duplicate**, **move toward an anchor**\n(slide-to-contact / one step / land inside), and learned **stencils**.\n\nThree details carry most of the weight:\n\n* **Relational colours.** A rule like \"paint the shape the colour of the lone\n  marker\" cannot use a literal, because the literal changes between examples.\n  Colours can be named by where they come from: the largest object, the\n  uniquely-coloured one, the *nearest object of a different colour*.\n\n* **Alternatives per group.** Local consistency cannot tell \"this object was\n  deleted\" from \"this object moved away\" — both leave background behind, and\n  deletion is cheaper, so it wins the group and the assembled rule then fails\n  globally. The cheapest assembly is tried first, then single substitutions\n  from each group's shortlist.\n\n* **A task-level prior.** When no demonstration ever removes anything, an action\n  that removes something is not a cheap explanation but a wrong one that looks\n  cheap locally, and is priced accordingly.\n\nCapacity guards: a table with nearly as many rows as training objects is\nrejected; a stencil that was only ever demonstrated once is rejected.\n\n### 2.4 Compositional object programs\n\n`engine/solvers/objchain.py` (new)\n\nObject operators as *seeds* are applied once. This is a dedicated search over\nchains of them, to depth 3, with observational-equivalence pruning and a fitted\ncolour map available as a final step. It finds programs like\n\n```\ncmap( recolour-largest-5( delete-squares( outline-majority-shape( input ) ) ) )\n```\n\nwhich is four object-level steps and unreachable anywhere else in the engine.\nIt is a separate module rather than a wider setting on the main enumerator\nbecause the two vocabularies have opposite economics: the object vocabulary is\nlarge but its branching collapses immediately (most selectors pick the same\nobjects, so most operators are observationally identical after one level),\nwhile the pixel library is the reverse. Mixing them at every depth multiplies\nboth branching factors for no gain.\n\n### 2.5 Composition in the other direction\n\n`engine/solvers/refine.py` (new)\n\n`cascade` composes forwards: a grid operator prepares the input, a specialist\nexplains the rest. The reverse — `output = correction(specialist(input))` — was\nnot covered anywhere, and it is a large family: a specialist that gets the\nstructure right and the finish wrong. Specialists are asked for candidates, the\nones that already fit are ignored, and the **near misses** become starting\nstates for a short search over corrections.\n\n### 2.6 Rules that branch on the input\n\n`engine/solvers/conditional.py` (new)\n\nEvery other hypothesis is a single total function, so tasks whose\ndemonstrations genuinely do different things are lost with an empty hypothesis\nset. A predicate partitions the pairs; each group is explained by an ordinary\nhypothesis refitted on that group alone.\n\nA branch is a licence to memorise, so the guards are severe: at least four\ntraining pairs; **every** group must carry at least two; a branch of three or\nmore pairs must survive its own leave-one-out refit; the branch rules must\nactually differ; and a task that some single hypothesis already explains is\nleft alone. On the development split these guards take the family from 89\n\"fitting\" tasks (nearly all of them memorisation) down to 2.\n\n### 2.7 Panel stacks combined by a learned table\n\n`engine/solvers/paneltable.py` (new)\n\n`partition` folds panels together through a closed list of boolean operators\nwith \"on\"/\"off\" colours from a short list. Instead of naming the operator, this\nlearns it: for each cell position the stack of panels supplies a key and the\noutput supplies the value. One conflict-free table subsumes all eight boolean\nops, priority overlays and arbitrary colour combinations, at any number of\npanels. Keys are offered at five levels of invariance (ordered, sorted, set,\ncount, count-plus-colour).\n\nA second family one level up **classifies** each panel and writes a learned\nblock for it, laid out the way the panels were, transposed, or strung into a\nrow or column.\n\n### 2.8 Continuing a pattern\n\n`engine/solvers/extend.py` (new)\n\n*Periodic completion*: part of the grid carries a repeating pattern and the rest\nis empty. The period is fitted against the content's bounding box with\nbackground taken at face value — treating background as *unknown* everywhere is\nwrong here, because the empty cells inside the drawn pattern are part of it —\nand the fill applies only beyond that box. The period is inferred **per grid**,\nbecause in real tasks it changes between examples.\n\n*Reflective extension*: a grid continued past its own edge by bouncing rather\nthan wrapping (`a b c b a b c`). Plain tiling gets this wrong at every seam.\n\n### 2.9 A general output-size law\n\n`engine/task.py` (extended)\n\nThe engine knew two shape laws, \"same size\" and \"an integer multiple\". Grids\nthat grow by a fixed border, shrink by one, or repeat a reflected pattern *k*\ntimes are affine in the input size and had no law at all; `Ctx.affine_shape`\nfits `out = a·in + b` with small integer coefficients.\n\n### 2.10 Locating the cut\n\n`engine/solvers/locate.py` (new)\n\nWhen the answer is a piece of the input, the problem factorises: find every\nplace the demonstrated output actually occurs inside its input — cheap, and it\ncompletely determines what a correct rule must return — then ask which of a\nlibrary of locators picks exactly those places in every pair at once. Locators\nname a rectangle by a colour's extent, an object's box or interior under several\nsegmentations and several readings of \"chosen\", or the fixed-size window that\nmaximises or minimises density / colour count / uniformity. Each dihedral image\nof the output is searched for as well, so the cut may be followed by a\ntransform.\n\n### 2.11 Assembly, self-stamping, tallies\n\n* `engine/solvers/assemble.py` — outputs built *from the objects* rather than\n  edited in the grid: overlaid, stacked in a sorted order, summarised one cell\n  per object, or selected by ordinal.\n* `engine/solvers/selfstamp.py` — the grid as its own brush. `geometry`'s\n  self-stamping gates only on \"is not background\"; the gate is widened to any\n  colour and to relational colours (the most common, the rarest, the most common\n  non-background), which is what tasks need when the gating colour changes\n  between examples. Plus offset repetition at a step that is *not* the grid size.\n* `engine/solvers/tally.py` — a count expressed as *how much of a fixed canvas\n  is filled*, which nothing that reasons from output dimensions can see; and\n  scattered marks gathered in reading order, including serpentine order.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The planner: a learned model in the solving loop\n\n`engine/planner.py` (new), `bench/train_planner.py` (new),\n`engine/portfolio.py` (rewritten scheduling)\n\n### What it is\n\nA sparse multinomial log-linear model over an action vocabulary of 34 hypothesis\nfamilies plus two control actions (`deepen`, `stop`):\n\n```\nP(a | s) = softmax_a( w_a · φ(s) )\n```\n\n`φ(s)` is the **reasoning state**, not just a description of the task:\n\n* *task descriptors* — shape law, palette change, separator lines, symmetry,\n  object counts, grid size;\n* *search history* — which families have already run and come back empty, how\n  many hypotheses currently fit, how much budget is left, which step this is;\n* *retrieved memory* — the families that solved the most similar previously\n  solved tasks, weighted by signature overlap and bucketed.\n\nThe retrieval block is a **feature block, not a shortcut**. Nothing in the\nplanner can answer a task by looking one up; it stores families and signatures,\nnever grids and never answers. Retrieval only shifts the logits, and the model\nis free to learn to distrust it:\n\n```\nlogits = W·φ_task(s) + W·φ_history(s) + W·φ_memory(s)\nP      = softmax(logits)\n```\n\n### How it is trained\n\nSupervision comes from the engine's own successes, never from the answer key.\nFor each development task the **last training pair is hidden**; every family is\nasked, independently, to explain the remaining demonstrations; a family that\nthen predicts the hidden pair correctly is the action the planner should have\nchosen. Test outputs are not read at any point in `bench/train_planner.py`, and\nthe held-out split is not read at all.\n\nFrom each (task, winning family) pair the state/action trajectory the planner\nwill actually meet is manufactured — `S0` with nothing tried, then states where\nsome other families have already come back empty — so the model learns not only\n\"which family suits this kind of task\" but \"which family suits it *given that\nthese have failed*\".\n\n### How it changes what the solver does\n\n`portfolio.solve` no longer walks a fixed list. While budget remains it asks\nthe planner for `P(action | state)`, runs the highest-probability family that\nhas not run yet, gives it a slice proportional to its probability, then\n**recomputes the distribution against the updated state** — which families have\nnow failed, how many hypotheses fit, how much time is left. Every family still\nruns; the planner moves time between them rather than silencing any, so a\nmistrained planner costs ordering, not coverage. The trace is recorded in\n`res.diagnostics[\"plan\"]`.\n\nThe planner proposes. It never decides whether a hypothesis is correct: every\nprogram is still executed against every training pair and discarded unless it\nreproduces all of them.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Results\n\nBoth runs: the 550 public task files in `data/arc/`, 20-second per-task budget,\n11 workers, identical scoring (a task counts only when **every** test pair is\nexact). `evidence/BASE_full.json` and `evidence/FINAL_full.json` carry the\nper-task records; `bench/report.py` seals each run with a SHA-256 over the task\ninputs scored and the per-task outcomes.\n\n| corpus | before | after | change |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| **ARC-AGI-1** (400) | 175 — **43.75%** | 197 — **49.25%** | **+22 tasks, +5.50 pts** |\n| **ARC-AGI-2** (150) | 45 — **30.00%** | 50 — **33.33%** | **+5 tasks, +3.33 pts** |\n| both (550) | 220 — 40.00% | 247 — 44.91% | +27 tasks, +4.91 pts |\n| ARC-AGI-1, two attempts | 186 — 46.50% | 204 — 51.00% | +18 tasks |\n| ARC-AGI-2, two attempts | 47 — 31.33% | 51 — 34.00% | +4 tasks |\n\n**Newly solved: 28. Regressed: 1** (`arc1_6ecd11f4`, and it comes back when the\nbudget is raised — see below).\n\n### The number to trust\n\nThe corpus was split by a hash of the task id into a development split (236\ntasks) and a held-out split (314). Every diagnostic, every decision about which\nfamily to write, and the planner's training data came from the development\nsplit only. The held-out split was never read during development.\n\n| split | before | after | change |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| development (236) — *architecture was shaped against these* | 86 — 36.4% | 107 — 45.3% | +21 |\n| **held-out (314)** — *never used* | 134 — 42.7% | **140 — 44.6%** | **+6** |\n| held-out, ARC-AGI-1 files (234) | 111 — 47.4% | 115 — 49.1% | +4 |\n| held-out, ARC-AGI-2 files (80) | 23 — 28.8% | 25 — 31.3% | +2 |\n\nThe gap is the honest finding of this pass: **+21 on tasks whose failures were\ninspected, +6 on tasks that were not.** The families are general in form -- none\nof them tests for a task -- but *which* families were worth writing was chosen\nby looking at development failures, and ARC's tail is diverse enough that this\ntransfers at roughly a third of the rate. The headline +5.5 / +3.3 is real and\nreproducible on this corpus; **+1.9 points is the better estimate of what this\npass would be worth on tasks nobody looked at.**\n\n### Diagnostics\n\n| | before | after |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| failures with **no fitted hypothesis at all** | 241 | 221 |\n| failures where something fit but nothing was right | 89 | 82 |\n| solver exceptions | 0 | 0 |\n\n### Runtime\n\n| | before | after |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| mean per task | 13.4 s | 17.2 s |\n| median | 14.9 s | 16.8 s |\n| 95th percentile | 19.6 s | 20.0 s |\n| total CPU (550 tasks) | 7 378 s | 9 448 s |\n| wall clock at 11 workers | 746 s | 868 s |\n\n+28% CPU for +12% solved. The engine now sits against its budget where it\npreviously had slack: raising the held-out budget from 20 s to 45 s adds two\nmore tasks (138 → 140 in an earlier revision) and recovers regressions caused by\ncontention, which was **not** true of the starting engine, where tripling the\nbudget bought a single task. Search is no longer free.\n\n### What wins\n\nWinning program prefixes in the final run show the new families carrying real\nweight rather than decorating the portfolio: `paneltable` 18 tasks, `objproc`\n13, `selfstamp` 9, `pdict` 4, `locate` 3.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Ablations\n\nHeld-out split (314 tasks), 20-second budget, one component removed per\nrun, everything else identical. The development split is not used here: a\ncomponent's contribution measured on the tasks that motivated it would be\nflattered by the same selection that put it in.\n\n| configuration | solved | rate | change | CPU |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| **full engine** | 140 / 314 | 44.59% | — | 5380 s |\n| without object process induction (`objproc`, `objchain`) | 135 / 314 | 42.99% | -5 | 5251 s |\n| without leave-one-out counterfactual pruning | 138 / 314 | 43.95% | -2 | 5558 s |\n| without the learned planner (fixed schedule) | 139 / 314 | 44.27% | -1 | 4855 s |\n| without object operators in the enumerator | 139 / 314 | 44.27% | -1 | 5223 s |\n| without deep composition (`refine`, `cascade`, `conditional`, `objchain`) | 139 / 314 | 44.27% | -1 | 4954 s |\n| without the new structure families (panel tables, self-stamping, continuation, tallies, locators, assembly) | 140 / 314 | 44.59% | +0 | 5393 s |\n\nRead plainly, on tasks nobody looked at:\n\n* **Object process induction is the one component that clearly pays** (+5).\n  It is also the most general thing added — an algebra over object actions with\n  a low-capacity learner on top, rather than a rule for a shape of task.\n* Leave-one-out pruning is worth +2, the planner, the object operators and deep\n  composition +1 each.\n* **The new structure families are worth nothing here** (+0), despite winning 18\n  tasks outright in the headline run as the cheapest fitting explanation. On\n  held-out tasks they are *redundant* rather than useless: remove them and older\n  families recover the same tasks. Their measured value came from the\n  development split, which is exactly the kind of gain a held-out split exists\n  to expose.\n\nThe honest reading of the whole table is that a +5.5 point headline decomposes\ninto one component that generalises and a tail of components that mostly\nre-explain what the portfolio could already reach.\n\n---\n\n## 6. What is still failing, in order\n\n`bench/diagnose.py` over all 550 files with the finished engine. Percentages are\nof the 306 tasks it does not solve.\n\n| remaining failure mode | tasks | share of failures |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| **Nothing fit the demonstrations at all** — no hypothesis in the whole portfolio reproduced the training pairs | 218 | 71% |\n| **Something fit, but the right rule was never generated** — a wrong-but-consistent rule won by default | 75 | 25% |\n| Right answer produced, but ranked second | 10 | 3% |\n| Right answer produced, ranked below second | 3 | 1% |\n\nNinety-six per cent of what remains is a **generation** problem, and the split\nis essentially unchanged from where this pass started — the engine solves more\ntasks, but the shape of its failure is the same. Nothing here will be fixed by\nbetter scoring, a bigger beam or a longer budget.\n\nBy the structure of the task, ordered by failure rate (tags computed from the\ndemonstrations only, so they describe what is being lost rather than why):\n\n| structure | tasks | failure rate |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| same-size in and out (in-place editing) | 367 | 64% |\n| grids with separator lines / lattices | 233 | 62% |\n| the same cells rearranged (object motion) | 38 | 45% |\n| the answer is a piece of the input | 122 | 41% |\n| output introduces colours not in the input | 179 | 50% |\n| only two demonstrations | 83 | 55% |\n| output is an integer upscale | 32 | 25% |\n| output is tiny (<= 9 cells) | 60 | 23% |\n\nThe concentration is in **in-place editing on lattice-structured grids with few\ndemonstrations** — which is also where ARC-AGI-2 lives, and where a rule is\nmost likely to be a multi-stage interaction between objects rather than one\ntransformation that any single family can name.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Honest limitations\n\n* **The planner is worth little on this engine.** It was built because the\n  architecture asked for it, and it does measurably beat chance at predicting\n  which family will generalise — but the diagnosis above explains why that\n  cannot translate into many tasks: reallocating a budget that is not the\n  binding constraint reallocates slack. Its measured contribution is in the\n  ablation table.\n\n* **`conditional` fires almost never.** Its guards are set where a branch is\n  trustworthy rather than where it is productive. Loosening them \"solves\" many\n  more tasks in the sense of fitting them, and generalises worse.\n\n* **Compute is spent to little effect.** The engine consumes most of its budget\n  on most tasks and would not solve more with three times as much. The\n  remaining failures need vocabulary, not seconds.\n\n* **ARC-AGI-3 is not supported.** The reasoning engine is separated from static\n  grid execution far enough that the planner and hypothesis machinery could be\n  reused in an observe–act loop, but no such loop exists here and none is\n  claimed.\n\n* **The corpora overlap.** 57 of the 150 ARC-AGI-2 files share an identifier\n  with an ARC-AGI-1 file, because the ARC-AGI-2 training set carries legacy\n  tasks forward. Both corpora are scored exactly as the harness defines them.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Reproducing every number\n\nRun from this directory (`astron/xyz`). Python 3.11+ and no third-party\npackages; `--jobs` is worker processes.\n\n```sh\n# 0. the engine's own unit tests (150 of them, including the no-leak boundary)\npython -m unittest discover -s tests\n\n# 1. the deterministic development / held-out split (pure function of task id)\npython bench/split.py --write\n\n# 2. baseline: the engine as supplied, no planner\npython bench/run_arc.py --budget 20 --jobs 11 --out evidence/BASE_full.json --quiet\n\n# 3. improved engine, planner active\npython bench/run_arc.py --budget 20 --jobs 11     --planner policy/arc_planner.json --out evidence/FINAL_full.json --quiet\n\n# 4. scored side by side, by corpus and by split, each run sealed\npython bench/report.py evidence/BASE_full.json evidence/FINAL_full.json     --out evidence/BEFORE_FINAL.json\n```\n\nDiagnostics and experiments:\n\n```sh\n# failure-mode census (re-solves collecting every prediction, not just the top)\npython bench/diagnose.py --tasks data/split_dev --budget 20 --jobs 11     --out evidence/DIAG_dev.json\n\n# is the synthesiser limited by time, depth or beam width?\npython bench/beam_probe.py --run evidence/FINAL_full.json --limit 40 --jobs 11\n\n# does more budget help?  (compare against the same tasks at --budget 20)\npython bench/run_arc.py --budget 60 --jobs 11 --tasks data/split_dev     --out evidence/DEV_b60.json --quiet\n```\n\nRetraining the planner (development split only; reads no test outputs):\n\n```sh\npython bench/train_planner.py --tasks data/split_dev --budget 0.5 --jobs 11\n```\n\nAblations (held-out split, one component removed per run):\n\n```sh\npython bench/ablate.py --tasks data/split_hold --budget 20 --jobs 11\n```\n\nTimings vary with the machine. The solved set does not, except for tasks\nsitting against their per-task deadline — and there are more of those than\nthere used to be, because the engine now uses its budget.\n\n### Files changed\n\nNew: `engine/objops.py`, `engine/planner.py`, `engine/solvers/objproc.py`,\n`objchain.py`, `refine.py`, `conditional.py`, `paneltable.py`, `selfstamp.py`,\n`extend.py`, `tally.py`, `locate.py`, `assemble.py`; `bench/split.py`,\n`diagnose.py`, `report.py`, `beam_probe.py`, `train_planner.py`, `ablate.py`;\n`policy/arc_planner.json`; this document.\n\nModified: `engine/portfolio.py` (planner-driven scheduling, module registry),\n`engine/enum_core.py` (object seeds), `engine/objects.py` (row/column\nsegmentations), `engine/task.py` (affine shape law), `engine/solvers/`\n`objects_map.py`, `cascade.py`, `objwise.py`, `panelwise.py`, `rewrite.py`\n(module lists, band-segmentation pricing), `bench/run_arc.py` (planner and\nablation flags, backward-compatible job tuple).\n"}, {"name": "ARC-V2-V3.md", "url": "/astron/xyz/ARC-V2-V3.md", "text": "# Version-space ranking (V2) and cross-fitted training (V3)\n\nThis document covers one pass over the ASTRA engine implementing the two\nspecifications supplied with it — `ARC_VERSION_SPACE_PREDICTIVE_EQUATION_V2.md`\nand `README_ASTRA_TRAINING_ARCHITECTURE_V3.md` — plus the expressive-reach work\nthat the engine's own diagnostics said was the only thing that could move the\nscore.\n\nEverything below was produced by the code in this directory on the 550 public\ntask files in `data/arc/`. No external model is consulted at any point, by this\ncode or by the process that wrote it; there is no network call, no API key and\nno language-model dependency anywhere in the engine.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the measurement said before anything was changed\n\nThe shipped engine solves **203/400 ARC-AGI-1 (50.75%)** and **51/150\nARC-AGI-2** here at a 20-second per-task budget with the shipped planner. Of its\n203 ARC-AGI-1 failures:\n\n| class | tasks |\n|---|---:|\n| nothing fit the demonstrations at all | 144 |\n| something fit, but no fitted rule produced the answer | 59 |\n\nand by shape law:\n\n| output shape vs input | unsolved | of which nothing fit |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| **same shape** | **157** | **106** |\n| shrinking | 35 | 28 |\n| growing | 11 | 9 |\n\nTwo things follow, and they decided the whole pass.\n\n**Ranking is not the bottleneck.** The engine's own `bench/diagnose.py` run\nrecorded exactly one development task where the correct answer was generated and\nranked below position two. A perfect ranker is worth about one task. So the V2\nequation was implemented because it is the right thing to have — it is what\nstops a *newly added* generator from poisoning the vote — not because it was\ngoing to be the score.\n\n**Expressive reach is the bottleneck, and it is concentrated in one place.**\n77% of unsolved ARC-AGI-1 tasks keep the input's shape, and two thirds of those\nhave nothing fitting at all.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What was added\n\n### 2.1 Typed programs: `engine/program.py`\n\nA `Hyp` stores an opaque closure. That is enough to *check* a rule and nothing\nelse, and the V2 equation asks a question that cannot be answered about a\nclosure: how many different parameterisations of this same abstraction also\nexplain the demonstrations, and do they agree about the test grid?\n\nSo a program is split in two:\n\n* `struct` — the canonical structure `c`: a nested tuple of operator names with\n  typed *holes* where literals belong. Two programs differing only in a colour\n  constant share one structure.\n* `theta` — the literals that fill those holes, in depth-first order.\n\nBoth halves are hashable, serialisable, and carry an explicit code length, so\n`l_Lambda(c)` and `l(theta|c)` are computed rather than asserted.\n\nOne correction to the specification was needed here. The V2 document treats\n`2^-l(c)` as a weight without saying where the code lengths come from; an\narbitrary hand-assigned cost table does not satisfy Kraft's inequality, and an\nMDL weighting over a code that no prefix code could realise is not an MDL\nweighting. Operator code lengths are therefore derived from a weight table and\nnormalised so the Kraft sum over the operator alphabet is **exactly 1**;\n`tests/test_program.py::test_operator_code_is_kraft_tight` asserts it.\n\n### 2.2 Typed synthesis: `engine/synthesis.py`\n\nA bounded bottom-up search over structures, kept beside the legacy enumerator\nrather than replacing it, so the ablation is one flag rather than two checkouts.\nThree things it does that the legacy search does not:\n\n* every candidate exposes `(c, theta)`;\n* **table-valued literals are solved, not enumerated** — a recolouring table is\n  read off the demonstrations cell by cell, which is what makes an operator\n  carrying a ten-entry table affordable at all;\n* a binary combination **re-enters the frontier**, so `u(binary(a, b))` is\n  reachable. The handoff's description of the legacy limitation was accurate and\n  this is the part that fixes it.\n\n### 2.3 Version-space predictive ranking: `engine/vspace.py`\n\nThe V2 equation, implemented as written:\n\n```\nSupport_j(y) = sum_c 2^-l(c) * 2^-lambda*R_B(c;D) * p_B(y | x*_j, c, D)\n```\n\nwith `p_B` the MDL-weighted mass that the discovered fitting parameterisations\nof `c` put on `y`, and `R_B` the capped cross-subset predictive regret. Caps,\nthe subset family `S_m`, `b_max`, `lambda` and `K_theta` are module constants\nand are reported in the diagnostics of every solve.\n\nFour disciplines from §13 of the specification are enforced rather than assumed:\n\n* **the hat is real** — `refit` searches a declared budget and the diagnostics\n  report `refits`, `truncated` and `empty_spaces` per task;\n* **structure search never reruns** — `c` is frozen and only its literals are\n  refitted against a subset;\n* **no test output is reachable** — only `ctx.train` pairs are handed to the\n  refitter;\n* **opaque candidates stay on legacy ranking** — a hypothesis takes part only if\n  it carries a real `Prog`. Nothing parses a name to invent parameters.\n\nThe integration into `portfolio.py` is exact rather than approximate. The\nexisting vote exponentiates `-score/2`, so a typed structure enters as one\nfurther family with `score = -2 ln(Support)`, which reproduces the V2 term with\nno rescaling. With no typed candidates present the ranking is bit-for-bit the\nincumbent's.\n\n### 2.4 Conjunctive cell rules: `engine/solvers/celltree.py`\n\nThis is where the score is.\n\n`cellwise` induces exact lookup tables, but each table is keyed by **one**\ncontext family at a time: the neighbourhood, *or* the parity, *or* the\ncontaining object's size. A rule needing two at once — \"a cell of the largest\nobject that also sits on the border becomes blue\" — is not expressible there at\nany table size. `celltree` learns a decision tree over a 60-feature bank\n(neighbourhood, position, rays in eight directions, ray *distances*, containing\nobject's size/shape-rank/holes/rectangularity, row and column statistics,\nmirror and periodic reads, betweenness); conjunction comes from depth.\n\nDepth is where the capacity risk is, so three guards apply, and the middle one\nturned out to matter more than everything else:\n\n1. **a smallest-tree-first ladder** — leaf budgets 3, 6, 12, ... are tried in\n   order and the first that explains the demonstrations wins;\n2. **delta targets** — the tree predicts *the difference from the input*, with\n   one `KEEP` leaf covering every untouched cell. Most same-shape ARC rules\n   leave most of the grid alone, and a tree that has to name the colour of every\n   untouched cell spends its leaf budget restating the input;\n3. **a held-out demonstration refit** — the tree is refitted with one\n   demonstration removed and must reproduce it, or it is charged three extra\n   bits.\n\nA fourth guard is really an expressiveness fix. A constant-leaf tree cannot\nexpress \"every mark paints to the right edge\": the leaf has to emit *the colour\nof the mark on this row*, which is a different colour in every row and therefore\nnot a constant at all, and the tree responds by memorising one leaf per row. So\na leaf may instead **copy a colour-valued feature** — a ray, a mirror, a\nperiodic read, a quadrant scan — for the features in its own bank. Symmetry\ncompletion, ray filling and cascades all become two- or three-split trees.\n\nMeasured on the 157 unsolved same-shape ARC-AGI-1 tasks, in isolation, at a\nfour-second budget, each row adding to the one above it:\n\n| version | tasks with a fitting tree | correct (top 2) |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| equality splits, 45 features | 29 | 5 |\n| + threshold splits, diagonal and betweenness features | 45 | 8 |\n| + **delta targets** | 58 | 13 |\n| + shape-rank and gap features, smallest-tree ladder | 60 | 15 |\n| + centre-relative position, ray+pos bank | 62 | 16 |\n| + quadrant-scan cascades, **copy leaves** | 66 | 20 |\n| + ordinal mark-rank features | 67 | 21 |\n| + per-grid background, 4-connectivity readings | 68 | 22 |\n| + seed-displacement features (\"draw\" bank) | 68 | 22 |\n| + lattice: panel coordinates and the exemplar panel | 71 | **23** |\n\nTwo of these were worth more than everything around them, and both were\nreductions in what the tree has to say rather than additions to what it can\nsee: delta targets (one leaf instead of one per untouched cell) and copy leaves\n(one leaf instead of one per distinct colour). The gap between 68 fitting and\n22 correct is the honest cost of the capacity that remains.\n\nThe last row is a negative result kept on purpose. Categorising the 136\nsame-shape tasks that survive this family shows the largest group by far — 78\nof them — is \"the output is the input plus something drawn on the background\",\nand what those rules say is a displacement from a mark: a motif stamped around\neach seed, a box one cell outside each shape. The features that expresses\n(signed offset to the nearest mark, offset inside the nearest bounding box,\ndiagonal betweenness) were added and bought nothing measurable on this set.\nThey are retained, because they are what that category needs and because the\ntree that will use them is a later change, but nothing here should be read as\ntheir having paid for themselves yet.\n\nLooking at those 78 tasks rather than at their category label is what produced\nthe last row. Most of them are not about cells at all: the grid is a lattice\nruled by separator lines, one panel carries a motif, and the answer copies it\ninto the others. Per cell that reads \"take the value at my own position inside\nmy panel, from the panel my band agrees on\" — one copy leaf over one feature,\nand unreachable without it however deep the tree. The exemplar requires the\nnon-empty panels to *agree* rather than requiring exactly one of them, because\nthese tasks usually have two or three panels already carrying the motif and\nuniqueness would refuse every one.\n\n### 2.5 Output-cell rules: `engine/solvers/canvastree.py`\n\nThe same machinery pointed at the other end of the map, for canvases whose shape\nis a fixed multiple (or divisor) of the input. Each output cell gets the source\ncell under division (the scaling read), under modulo (the tiling read), both\nunder the four reflections, and its coordinates inside the repeating block —\nwhich is what makes mirrored tilings and fractal stamps reachable without\nenumerating tiling programs.\n\n### 2.5b Iterated cell rules: `engine/solvers/growtree.py`\n\nA cell tree learns one function from a cell's neighbourhood to its output\ncolour and applies it *once*. That is the wrong shape for rays, trails,\nspirals, gravity and flood fill, because a single pass has to know the answer\nat every distance from the source at once: \"paint the diagonal from the mark to\nthe edge\" becomes a separate case for every length of diagonal the tree has\nseen, and the first longer one in the test grid is wrong.\n\nStated as a process the same rule is one leaf — *if my upper-left neighbour is\ncoloured and I am background, take its colour* — and run to quiescence the\ndiagonal is any length at all. This is the reduction that delta targets and\ncopy leaves were, applied to time rather than to the grid: the tree says less,\nso what it says generalises.\n\nThe rounds of the process are not in the data; only the first and last states\nare demonstrated. They are recovered by **layering the difference**. A changed\ncell may go in this round if it touches a cell that is already settled *and\ncarries a colour*. That the source must be coloured is the whole of it: on a\ndiagonal ray every cell also touches settled background, so the weaker \"any\nsettled neighbour\" test paints the entire ray in one round and recovers nothing\nabout the process. With the coloured-source test the ray walks outward one cell\nper round, which is the rule. The ordering is canonical — no search, no choice\n— so the state sequence follows from each demonstration directly:\n\n    s_0 = input,   s_k = input with rounds 1..k painted,   s_L = output\n\nEvery consecutive pair is another demonstration of the *same* rule, which is\nthe evidence multiplier: three grids and a twelve-deep ray give the tree\nthirty-six rounds, not three. A final round from the output to itself is\nincluded on purpose — without it nothing in the training data says the process\never stops, and a rule that never reaches quiescence is not a rule.\n\nFour guards, in order of how much they reject:\n\n* the layering must exist at all, and be at least two rounds deep — one round\n  is an ordinary cell tree and `celltree` already has it;\n* the fitted rule is **run**, from each training input, and must reproduce that\n  training output exactly. The layered rows are a surrogate for the process,\n  and fitting them is not the same as the process working;\n* it must converge: a rule still changing cells at the iteration cap is\n  rejected rather than truncated;\n* and `celltree`'s held-out refit applies unchanged — drop one demonstration,\n  refit, *run the process*, require the dropped grid back.\n\nThe intermediate states are sampled (at most ten per demonstration, always\nincluding the first rounds and the last) because computing 141 features per\nstate is what dominates the cost. The sampling is safe precisely because of the\nsecond guard: the verification run exercises every round whether it was sampled\nor not.\n\n### 2.5e Repeated motifs: `engine/solvers/repeat.py`\n\nA shape that marches across the grid — the same block every four columns, a\nfigure stepping diagonally into the corner, a stamp walking toward the marker\nthat names its direction — is a family neither tree can state. A one-pass cell\nrule cannot: the *n*-th copy is at a distance no neighbourhood sees, so the\ntree enumerates a case per copy and the first longer grid is wrong. The\niterated rule cannot either, because the copies do not touch — nothing local\nconnects one to the next.\n\nWhat connects them is a **step vector**, and the whole discipline of the module\nis that the step is *derived*, never searched. A free search over displacements\nwould find a step for almost any pair of grids and explain nothing at all. The\nderivations are a closed vocabulary, each a function of the input alone:\n\n* the motif's own bounding box, so copies abut — eight directions;\n* the bounding box plus one, so copies are separated by a single cell;\n* one cell, which is a smear rather than a repetition;\n* the displacement to its nearest neighbour, and the reverse;\n* and its own extent *pointed at* that neighbour, which is how \"repeat toward\n  the marker\" is stated without the rule naming a direction at all.\n\nWhich objects repeat is likewise a selector from a closed list (all, the\nlargest, the ones whose shape is unique, the ones bigger than a cell) and the\ncolour of the copies is either the motif's own or its marker's. The guard is\nreconstruction against every demonstration, plus one more: a rule that paints\n*nothing* — every copy lands outside the grid — fits any task whose output is\nits input, and is rejected for saying nothing.\n\n### 2.5d Per-object trees: `engine/solvers/objtree.py`\n\n`objects_map` already learns \"which colour does each object become\", and it is\ndeliberately low-capacity: one feature, or two, and no more. That guard is\nright — with a dozen training objects any rich feature set separates them by\naccident — but it is enforced by a *cap* rather than by a *cost*, so a rule\nthat genuinely needs three tests is not merely disfavoured, it is unreachable.\n\"Delete the shapes that touch the border, recolour the square ones red and the\nrest blue\" is three tests and nothing in the portfolio could say it.\n\nThis is the `celltree` induction pointed at objects instead of cells, with\ndescription length as the guard — the guard the rest of the engine uses. A\nthird test has to pay for itself in bits, and the smallest-tree-first ladder\nstill finds the two-test version when one exists.\n\nThe action alphabet is an **integer code**, and that choice is what makes copy\nleaves keep working — which is the most valuable part of the module. A\nrecolouring is coded *as the colour itself*, so a leaf that copies a\ncolour-valued object feature says \"recolour each object to the colour of the\nthing containing it\", or \"to the colour of its nearest neighbour\": relational\nrules that no constant leaf can state. The rest of the alphabet is keep,\ndelete, fill the bounding box, fill the interior holes, outline, and slide\nuntil blocked in each of the four directions.\n\nTwo ambiguities are not resolvable by priority and are handled by trying both\nreadings rather than by guessing: a shape that falls further than its own\nheight leaves all of its original cells background, which reads exactly like an\nerasure. Both labellings are derived and the rebuild decides between them.\n\nThree guards:\n\n* the labels must *compose*. Each object's action is read off the output, and\n  then the whole grid is **rebuilt** from the labels and compared exactly.\n  Deriving a label per object is not the same as the labels composing, and\n  overlapping paints are where that breaks;\n* a fitted tree is checked the same way, by rebuilding, not by label accuracy;\n* and the held-out refit: drop a demonstration, refit, rebuild the dropped\n  grid, require it exactly.\n\nFive segmentations crossed with two label readings would be sixty trees per\ntask, and most of those segmentations agree — a task whose objects are\nsingle-coloured and not diagonally touching looks identical under all of them.\nA reading already seen is skipped rather than re-fitted.\n\n### 2.5c Diagonal coordinates in `celltree`\n\nA decision tree tests one feature at a time, so a rule stated as a relation\n*between* two features is not reachable at all. \"The cell is on the diagonal\nthrough the mark\" is `r - seed_r == c - seed_c`, and no sequence of tests on\n`seed_dr` and `seed_dc` separately says it — the tree has to name every offset\npair one at a time and then fails on the first longer diagonal it has not seen.\nFive features close that: `r-c` and `r+c` (constant along the grid's own\ndiagonals), `r+c_from_end` (the same anchored to the bottom-left corner, so it\nmeans one thing across grids of different sizes), and `seed_diag` /\n`seed_anti`, the difference and sum of the displacement from the nearest mark,\nwhich turn the whole family into a single equality test against zero. They are\ncomputed from the *raw* displacement rather than the clamped pair `seed_dr` and\n`seed_dc` use, because the point is to stay exact far from the seed — which is\nexactly where a clamped offset stops distinguishing one diagonal from the next.\n\n### 2.6 The tree as a *correction*: `refine.py`, `cascade.py`, `rewrite.py`, `conditional.py`\n\n`refine` already composed \"specialist, then a short operator search\". A\ncorrection is not always an operator chain: when the specialist lands on the\nright canvas, what is left is usually a rule about individual cells, and that is\na conjunctive cell rule. `refine` now fits one directly from\n`(specialist output, true output)` pairs, and the three other meta-solvers carry\n`celltree` in their specialist lists for the mirror-image reason — a re-posing\nthat fixes the canvas turns a task the cell rule cannot see into one it can.\n\n---\n\n## 3. V3 training\n\n### 3.1 Canonical content groups: `bench/split.py`\n\nThe shipped split hashes the task *id*. The bundled corpora overlap: **11 task\npairs are the same underlying task under two ids**, and an id split separates\nthem, which puts the answer to a held-out task in the fitting half. Content\ngroups are now keyed by the demonstrations themselves, reduced to canonical form\nunder the dihedral group and under colour renaming; `partition()` and the new\n`folds(k)` move whole groups.\n\n### 3.2 Library learning: `engine/library.py`\n\n`J_lib(Lambda; T) = l(Lambda) - sum_t w_t log2 Z_t(Lambda)` with\n`Z_t = sum_h 2^-L_t(h)` over canonical verified structures. Aliases are folded\nbefore the sum, so a generator emitting one structure by two routes cannot\ninflate the evidence, and `supp(m)` counts **distinct content groups**, so\nduplicated files cannot manufacture a reusable abstraction. A macro must clear\nboth `supp(m) >= r_min` and `Delta_lib(m) > 0` before it reaches cross-fold\nvalidation.\n\nAn adopted library changes two things, which is what makes it a library rather\nthan a statistic: `vspace` charges macro occurrences once when it computes\n`l_Lambda(c)`, and `synthesis` gains one composite operator per macro, so a\ndepth-`d` search over `n`-step macros reaches programs of length up to `d*n`.\n\nThis replaces name-string n-gram mining for the typed path. The old\n`Policy.abstractions` field is untouched and still drives the legacy enumerator;\n`Policy.library` is new and separate, because conflating \"operator names that\nco-occur in a program string\" with \"fragments of a typed AST\" is exactly the\nthing §6 of the handoff forbids.\n\n### 3.3 Soft search targets: `bench/train_planner.py`, `engine/planner.py`\n\n`q_t(a|s) ∝ sum_h 2^-E_t(h)` with `E_t(h) = L_t(h) + kappa log2(1 + W_t(h)/W_0)`.\nThe probe now records, per verified program, its cost and the work that found\nit, so both halves of `E_t` are measured rather than reconstructed.\n`Planner.fit_soft` minimises `KL(q || pi)` — whose gradient is `pi - q`, making\nthe incumbent's hard-target fit the special case where `q` is a point mass — plus\nan optional `eta KL(pi || pi_ref)` stability term.\n\n### 3.4 Exploration floor\n\n`pi~ = (1-eps) pi + eps u` over the *valid* actions, applied in\n`portfolio._planned_generation`. A policy trained on its own successes can drive\na family's share to zero and then never see the evidence that would bring it\nback; the floor bounds how far one round can narrow the search.\n\n### 3.5 Cross-fitted adoption: `bench/evolve.py --cross-fit K`\n\nPropose on `T_k`, freeze, measure on `V_k`, with a lexicographic gate: solve rate\nfirst, `min_k dS_k >= -delta_max` so one collapsed fold cannot be averaged away,\nand search work only as a tie-breaker at equal accuracy. Approval retrains the\nfrozen recipe once on all permitted groups; rejection leaves the incumbent\nuntouched.\n\n---\n\n## 4. What is deliberately *not* claimed\n\n* The version-space quantities are **search-conditional**. They are not\n  posteriors over all fitting programs, and the notation `Theta_B` is kept in\n  the code and the diagnostics for that reason.\n* `lambda`, `b_max`, `K_theta`, `kappa`, `eps`, `r_min`, `delta_max` and\n  `omega_min` are frozen module constants chosen before measurement. They have\n  not been swept.\n* The cross-fitted gate estimates transfer between content groups of the same\n  corpus. It is not evidence about a sealed evaluation set.\n* One deliberate departure from §16 of the V2 document. It says an empty\n  version space contributes zero answer support, and at the level the equation\n  describes it does — `predictive` over no parameterisations is the empty\n  distribution, and there is a test for it. But when the *bounded refitter*\n  declines a structure whose concrete fit is already in hand (its literal\n  domain exceeds the declared work cap, say), dropping a verified program would\n  be reporting a search limit as evidence about the rule. Those structures\n  contribute the parameterisations already discovered, and the diagnostics\n  count them under `empty_spaces`.\n* `W_t` is **measured wall-clock seconds, not a deterministic primitive\n  count**. The V3 document assumes the latter; this engine is time-bounded\n  throughout, so no reproducible counter exists inside it. The deviation is\n  stated rather than papered over, and the adoption gate is built so it cannot\n  matter much: work is only ever a tie-breaker at equal solve rate. A genuinely\n  deterministic mode would need a work budget replacing the time budget in the\n  portfolio, which is a larger change than this pass.\n* No component is claimed to have improved the score except where §5 reports a\n  measured before/after on identical task files at an identical budget.\n\n---\n\n## 4j. The diagonal features pay; the iterated family, as first configured, did not\n\nFull corpus, 550 files, 20-second budget, same planner and scorer, against the\nrun recorded in `evidence/V2_AFTER.json`. The candidate adds the five diagonal\ncoordinates to `celltree` and the iterated family `growtree`:\n\n| | before | after |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| ARC-AGI-1 top 1 | 224 | 224 |\n| ARC-AGI-1 top 2 | 238 | 238 |\n| all 550 top 1 | 278 | 278 |\n| tasks where nothing fits | 142 | **133** |\n| wall | 2115 s | 2138 s |\n\nTwo won, two lost, sign test p = 0.688. **Net zero**, and the aggregate would\nhave said nothing more than that. What the per-task records say is worth more\nthan the total:\n\n| task | before | after |\n|---|---|---|\n| `arc1_3bd67248` | nothing fit at all | **`treeD[local+pos,3]`** |\n| `arc1_d0f5fe59` | `refine:…>>tree[pos,3]`, wrong | **`refine:…>>tree[pos,1]`**, right |\n| `arc1_32597951` | `treeD[local,6]`, right | `ca_n4_lax`, wrong |\n| `arc1_dbc1a6ce` | `connect_c($,8)`, right | `treeD[field,2]`, wrong |\n\nBoth wins are the **diagonal features**, and neither is the iterated family.\n`3bd67248` is the anti-diagonal-from-the-corner task that motivated them, and\nit now fits where nothing fit before. `d0f5fe59` is the more interesting one:\nthe tree that gets it right is *smaller* than the one that got it wrong —\nthree splits down to one. That is the signature this engine's real wins have\nalways had. The tree says less, so what it says generalises.\n\nBoth losses are `growtree`'s, and only one of them is about ranking.\n`arc1_32597951` is pure **starvation**: its candidate count fell from 19 to 18\nand the missing one is exactly the `treeD[local,6]` that used to solve it. The\ncell tree's slice shrank because another family was in front of it, and the\ntask was lost without any hypothesis ever competing for it. `arc1_dbc1a6ce` is\na rank shift — `typed`'s `connect_c($,8)` was displaced by a two-split cell\ntree — and it keeps the right answer at rank two, so at ARC's two-attempt\nscoring only `32597951` is a real loss.\n\nSo the finding is not \"the iterated family does not work\" — it reaches\n`arc1_3bd67248`, `arc1_7ddcd7ec` and `arc1_db3e9e38` on its own when probed\nwith `bench/family_probe.py` — it is that **a family which fits rarely has to\nbe cheap, or the families it displaces pay for it**. Its slice was 1.6 seconds\nand its most expensive bank was the full 141-feature one, at the end of that\nslice, over tens of thousands of rows. Both are now trimmed: 0.8 seconds, and\nthe full bank is gone from its list. That costs it one task it could reach\noffline and is the right trade, because the task it was starving was one the\nengine already had.\n\n## 4l. The second run, with the trim and the object tree\n\nSame files, same budget, same planner, against the same recorded baseline:\n\n| | `V2_AFTER` | `V3_FINAL` |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| ARC-AGI-1 top 1 | 224 | **225** |\n| ARC-AGI-1 top 2 | 238 | **240** |\n| ARC-AGI-2 top 1 | 54 | **55** |\n| all 550 top 1 | 278 | **280** |\n| all 550 top 2 | 294 | **296** |\n| tasks where nothing fits | 142 | 134 |\n| wall | 2115 s | 2149 s |\n\nFive won, three lost, sign test p = 0.363 — which on its own is not evidence of\nanything. What makes it evidence is that **the wins are the tasks the\nstandalone probe named in advance**:\n\n| task | won by | predicted by |\n|---|---|---|\n| `arc1_776ffc46` | `objtree[c4/all,4]` | `family_probe`, `objtree` |\n| `arc1_b27ca6d3` | `objtree[c4/size,1]` | `family_probe`, `objtree` |\n| `arc1_3bd67248` | `treeD[local+pos,3]` | the diagonal features |\n| `arc1_d0f5fe59` | `refine:…>>tree[pos,1]` | the diagonal features |\n| `arc2_21f83797` | — | not predicted |\n\nA p-value over eight coin flips says little; a family that names two tasks\nbefore the run and then wins exactly those two is a different kind of claim.\n\nThe three losses are worth as much as the wins. `arc1_44d8ac46` went to\n**`objtree`'s own overfit** — a three-split object tree outranking a correct\n`compose` hypothesis. That is the capacity problem the module was built to\navoid, arriving from the other direction: a cell tree's split is supported by\nhundreds of cells, an object tree's by a dozen objects, and the two do not\ndeserve the same cost floor. `arc1_32597951` is still the starvation loss and\n`arc1_dbc1a6ce` still the rank shift, both from the first run, and the trim did\nnot recover either — which is what motivates the ablation in §4m.\n\n## 4m. The ablation, and a guard of my own that was wrong\n\n`--drop growtree`, everything else identical:\n\n| | with | without |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| ARC-AGI-1 top 1 | 225 | **226** |\n| all 550 top 1 | 280 | **281** |\n| tasks where nothing fits | 134 | 134 |\n\nOne gained (`arc1_dbc1a6ce`, back to `typed`'s `connect_c($,8)`), none lost.\nThe iterated family is a **net negative in the portfolio**: it reached six\ntasks' candidate lists and won none, and the first of the two tasks it reaches\non its own the cell tree now takes anyway from the diagonal coordinates. Nine\nof its eleven fits are consistent rules that are not the task's rule, and a\ngenerator like that pays for its slice in other families' losses. It is stood\ndown; the module and its tests stay, because the layering recovers a process\nfrom two demonstrated states and that is worth having on record.\n\nThe ablation also **reattributes the starvation**. `arc1_32597951` is lost\nwithout `growtree` too, so the slice that displaced its correct\n`treeD[local,6]` was `objtree`'s. That one is cheap to fix honestly — the whole\nfamily probes 162 tasks in under a second, so its slice went from 1.2 s to\n0.3 s.\n\nThen I got one wrong, and the measurement caught it. The argument was that a\ncell tree's split is supported by hundreds of cells and an object tree's by a\ndozen objects, so an object tree with few objects behind each leaf should pay\nextra. It is wrong, for a reason worth keeping: **a penalty in leaves is a\npenalty in depth, and description length already charges for depth**. Charging\ntwice does not disfavour memorisation, it disfavours deep trees — and on\n`arc1_44d8ac46`, the very task the guard was built for, the correct rule *is*\nthe deeper one. `objtree` has that answer: a four-split `c4/shape` tree at cost\n9.68. Two three-split trees that are wrong sit below it at 7.75 and 7.83, and\nthe penalty (bigger for four leaves than three) pushed the right one further\ndown, out of the top two entirely.\n\n| task | without the charge | with it |\n|---|---|---|\n| `arc1_44d8ac46` | correct at rank 2 | correct below rank 2 |\n| `arc2_1d61978c` | — | correct at rank 2 |\n\nA one-for-one trade across corpora, and the wrong way round for the corpus\nthat is the target. The charge is reverted. The number is still computed and\nreported, because it is the right thing to look at when an object tree is\nsuspect; it is simply not added to the cost.\n\n## 4k. What each new family reaches on its own\n\n`bench/family_probe.py`, against the 162 ARC-AGI-1 tasks the recorded run still\nmisses, each family alone with no portfolio around it, two attempts, six\nseconds:\n\n| family | fits | correct | seconds | which |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---|\n| `growtree` | 11 | 2 | 98 | `3bd67248`, `7ddcd7ec` |\n| `objtree` | 5 | **2** | **0** | `776ffc46`, `b27ca6d3` |\n| `repeat` | 0 | 0 | 3 | — |\n\nThe gap between *fits* and *correct* is the number to read. `growtree` fits\neleven and is right about two: nine of its explanations are consistent rules\nthat are not the task's rule, and that ratio is why it has to be cheap.\n`objtree` fits five and is right about two, at a cost that does not register on\na stopwatch — five segmentations and six banks over a few dozen objects is\nnothing next to a hundred and forty-one features over every cell.\n\n`repeat` is a clean negative. Over the whole 400 it fits **two** tasks and is\nright about one, which the engine already solves. The derived-step vocabulary\nis too narrow for the tasks it was built for — those need a direction *per\nobject* taken from a marker, copies recoloured per marker, and steps that are\nnot the motif's bounding box at all. Widening the vocabulary is exactly the\nmove that turned the per-object stencil table into a memoriser, so it was not\nmade. The family is stood down from the portfolio and kept, with its tests, for\nthe record.\n\n## 4n. Merging GPT-6's ARC-1 work, and the hook it removed\n\n`delta_stencils` is a real gain and the reason it works is worth stating,\nbecause I had retired the idea on measurement. My version was a table from an\nobject key to the patch it becomes: realisable on 51 of 119 unsolved tasks,\nsurviving the held-out refit on 9, and solving **0**. GPT-6's is not a table of\npatches but a set of **sparse offsets**, each required to agree at *every*\ntraining instance of its seed key and to explain at least two observed changes,\nkept by a deterministic set cover, with foreground treated as an occluder and\nconflicting stamps causing abstention rather than an order-dependent answer.\nThat is the difference between a lookup and a rule, and it shows:\n\n| | V3_SHIP | V4_MERGED |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| ARC-AGI-1 top 1 | 226 | **227** |\n| ARC-AGI-1 top 2 | 240 | **241** |\n| all 550 top 1 | 281 | **282** |\n\nThree won -- `arc1_29c11459`, `arc1_3ac3eb23`, `arc1_8eb1be9a`, every one of\nthem a `subst_delta_*` -- and two lost.\n\n**One of the two losses was not GPT-6's solver but a hook their diff removed.**\n`arc1_dbc1a6ce` was solved by a *typed* program, `connect_c($,8)`, and their\n`synthesis.py`, `typed.py` and `learn.py` are each a pure deletion of the same\nthing: `synthesis.OP_BIAS`, the `prior=` argument `typed.py` passes into the\nsearch, and the two blocks in `learn.py` that install and clear it. That hook is\nthe only path by which anything learned reaches the typed enumerator. Without\nit the typed frontier is ordered by code length alone, and the policy and the\nlanguage model both express themselves as an opinion about operators that would\nreach the legacy enumerator and stop there. It changes nothing about what\n*fits* -- every candidate is still checked against every demonstration -- and\neverything about what is reached inside the budget, which is what a depth-three\ntyped program lives or dies by. Restored.\n\nThe JavaScript port still carried the hook, which is how a removal like this is\ncaught rather than argued about: the two ports would otherwise have diverged in\nsilence.\n\nI also had the diagnosis wrong first. `n_fit` fell on both lost tasks, so I\nassumed `delta_stencils` was crowding the others out. `bench/module_cost.py`\nsays `substitute` takes **0.1% of the clock at 138 fits per second**. It was not\ncrowding anything, and the typed diff was the actual cause.\n\n## 4c. What the regressions actually are\n\nSix tasks the incumbent solves at top 1 and this engine does not. Re-running\nthem with the full ranked list shows the shape of the problem plainly: in five\nof the six the correct answer is **at rank 2**, displaced by a tree. Under\nARC's two-attempt scoring those five are still solved, and the top-2 numbers in\n`RESULTS-V2-V3.md` say so. The sixth, `arc1_46f33fce`, produces no fitting\nhypothesis that reaches the answer at all; it was already the known casualty of\nthe previous pass, recorded in `RESULTS.md`.\n\n## 4d. What the ablations say, including what they say against this work\n\nHeld-out split, 314 tasks, everything fixed but the one part named. The\nincumbent scores **143 top 1 / 147 top 2** there; the engine after this pass\nscores **158 / 168**.\n\n| configuration | solved | top 2 | nothing fitting | vs full |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| full (R3) | 158 | 168 | 77 | — |\n| R0 legacy rank | 158 | 168 | 78 | +0 |\n| R1 canonical MDL | 159 | 169 | 78 | +1 |\n| R2 version space | 158 | 167 | 81 | +0 |\n| no_cell_tree | 159 | 166 | 83 | +1 |\n| no_canvas_tree | 158 | 167 | 77 | +0 |\n| no_typed_synthesis | 158 | 168 | 77 | +0 |\n| no_tree_families | 161 | 167 | 77 | +3 |\n\nTwo results here are against the work, and both are worth more than the\nheadline.\n\n**The version-space ranker is worth nothing.** R0 through R3 differ by at most\none task in 314, which is inside the noise of a wall-clock-bounded engine on a\nshared machine. The equation is implemented as specified — the tests pin every\nsanity check in §16 of the document, the code lengths satisfy Kraft exactly,\nand the diagnostics report every truncation — and it does not move the score.\nThat is not a surprise in hindsight: the engine's own `bench/diagnose.py` had\nalready found exactly one development task where the right answer was generated\nand ranked below position two. A perfect ranker was worth about one task before\nany of this was written, and it still is. What the ranker is for is the case it\nwas built for — keeping a newly added, very expressive generator from poisoning\nthe vote — and this corpus does not contain enough of that case to measure.\n\n**The standalone tree families cost three tasks at top 1.** Removing\n`celltree`, `canvastree` and `typed` as families *raises* the solve rate.\nLooking at which tasks move says why, and it is not that the trees are wrong:\nthe two tasks only `full` solves are both won by a tree, and the five only\n`no_tree_families` solves are won by `compose`, `objects` and `colormap` —\nfamilies that ran out of time when three more modules were sharing the budget.\nIt is a time-starvation result, not a capability result. The same tree\nmachinery is still reached in that configuration through `refine`, `cascade`,\n`rewrite` and `conditional`, which is why dropping the families costs so little\ncoverage (top 2 falls by one).\n\nThe honest reading is that at a 20-second budget this engine is now scheduling\nbound rather than reach bound, which is the opposite of where it was when this\npass started.  Two further runs test that, and the fix follows from them.\n\n| configuration | budget | solved | top 2 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| full | 20 s | 158 | 168 |\n| no_tree_families | 20 s | 161 | 167 |\n| full | **45 s** | 160 | 170 |\n| full + `MAX_SLICE` | 20 s | **160** | **169** |\n\nMore than doubling the budget buys two tasks. So the engine is not short of\ncompute in aggregate; what it is short of is compute *in the families that\nconvert it*. The tree families enumerate a fixed, finite set of fits -- a\nladder of leaf budgets over a fixed list of feature banks -- and cannot use a\nlarger share however generous the schedule is, but they were being handed one\nanyway, and it came out of `compose`, `objects` and `colormap`.\n\nSo a generator may now declare `MAX_SLICE`: the wall-clock beyond which it has\nnothing further to offer. The remainder is not reserved for anything; the\nscheduler recomputes the clock after every generator, so it simply falls\nthrough to the rest. At the original budget that recovers two of the three\ntasks the families were costing **and** keeps the coverage that removing them\nwould lose -- it reaches the 45-second run's solve rate without the 45 seconds.\n\nThis is the most useful thing the ablations produced, and it is a scheduling\nresult rather than a reasoning one. It would not have been visible without\nrunning the ablation that made the architecture look bad.\n\n## 4e. What the library objective did when it was actually run\n\nFitting `J_lib` on the development half of the records (236 tasks, the\nheld-out 314 untouched) adopts **no macros at all**, and the reason is the\nobjective working rather than failing.\n\n26 tasks emit a typed structure; 39 structures in total. The most reused spine\nfragment is `recolor_by(pick_crop(...))`, present in three distinct content\ngroups, so it clears the support floor. Adopting it shortens every structure\nthat contains it from 19.92 bits to 11.21 — a real 8.7 bits an occurrence\nagainst a library cost of 13.7. It is still refused, because\n\n```\narc1_0b148d64   pick_crop($,?K,?S)                        l(c)=12.24  l(th)= 5.64  R=0.35\n                recolor_by(pick_crop($,?K,?S),?K,?Y,?M)   l(c)=19.92  l(th)=32.25  R=6.00\n```\n\nand `Z_t` is a sum over verified structures dominated by its cheapest term.\nEach of those three tasks already has an 18-bit explanation; shortening a\n58-bit alternative by nine bits moves `log2 Z_t` by almost nothing, and the\nlibrary still has to be paid for. The fragment's only occurrences are in\nexplanations the demonstrations do not support — note `R = 6.00`, the capped\nmaximum, on every one of them: those structures predict withheld\ndemonstrations no better than chance.\n\nThat is the guard in §5 of the V3 document doing its job, and it is worth\nstating plainly because the alternative reading — \"library learning is\nimplemented but produced nothing, so it is probably broken\" — is the one a\nreader would otherwise reach. It produced nothing *here*. A corpus where a\nreusable fragment appears in tasks' best explanations, rather than in their\ndiscarded ones, is what it would take, and 26 typed solutions is not that\ncorpus.\n\n## 4f. Where the budget actually goes\n\n`bench/module_cost.py` reads the per-generator accounting out of a single\nbenchmark record, so this no longer costs one full run per family to ask.\nOn the 550-task record:\n\n| generator | share of clock | s/task | fits per second |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| cascade | 25.1% | 3.38 | 2.0 |\n| enumerate_dsl | 21.9% | 2.95 | 0.2 |\n| rewrite | 17.1% | 2.31 | 2.6 |\n| deep enumerator | 12.0% | 1.62 | 0.3 |\n| objchain | 7.9% | 1.07 | 0.2 |\n| refine | 6.1% | 0.82 | 2.9 |\n| celltree | 0.8% | 0.11 | 26.0 |\n\nFive generators take 84% of the clock. The general enumerator and its deep pass\ntake a third of it between them and win four tasks; the conjunctive cell rules\nreturn two orders of magnitude more fits per second for eight tenths of one per\ncent of the budget.\n\nThe obvious move is the wrong one. Those are real searches: their yield is low\nbecause they are the fallback that runs when nothing else has an idea, and more\ntime genuinely helps them, which is exactly the case `MAX_SLICE` must not be\napplied to. What the table actually argues for is *allocation* — the planner\ndeciding to spend less there on tasks that do not look like enumerator tasks —\nand that is what a learned policy is for. §4g measures one.\n\nTwo other rows in the results are worth reading together with this. Candidate\ncoverage moved from 212 tasks with nothing fitting to 142: the reach work did\nwhat it was for. And by family, `objects` fell from 46 solves to 32 while\n`cellwise` rose from 29 to 61 — the new families are not only solving tasks\nnothing solved, they are also winning the vote on tasks `objects` used to win.\nTwenty-nine net wins says that trade is mostly right, and the five regressions\nare where it is not.\n\n## 4g. The learned policy does not transfer\n\nFitted from the development half of the records alone — 236 tasks, 32\nsignature-conditioned family biases, 20 signatures with a learned skip, no\nmacros — and measured on the held-out 314 under the same code, budget and\nplanner:\n\n| | top 1 | top 2 |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| no policy | 161 | 168 |\n| learned policy | **156** | 166 |\n\nZero wins and five losses. The V3 adoption gate rejects it on\n`Delta S_CF > 0` at the first hurdle, and the default stays \"no learned\npolicy\", which is what the incumbent already shipped.\n\nIt is worth being precise about what this does and does not say. It does not\nsay learned scheduling cannot work — the fitted skips are the crude\ninstrument, a family with no recorded solve for a signature is dropped\nentirely rather than de-prioritised, and a family that solves nothing on 236\ndevelopment tasks can still be the only thing that solves a held-out one. It\nsays *this* policy, fitted this way, on this much evidence, transfers\nnegatively, and that the loop built to notice that noticed it. A gate that\nonly ever approves is not a gate.\n\n## 4h. Low yield per second is not low value\n\nThe cost table invites one more obvious move: `objchain` takes 7.9% of the\nclock at 0.17 fits per second, the worst ratio of any large consumer. Dropping\nit on the held-out split:\n\n| | top 1 | top 2 |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| with `objchain` | 161 | 168 |\n| without | 158 | 166 |\n\nZero wins, three losses. Its eight per cent is earning its keep, and the\nwarning in §4f was the right one: a generator that fits rarely is not thereby\nwasteful. It is the one that has an idea when nothing else does, and its fits\nare worth more each than a family that produces a thousand of them.\n\nThree attempts to spend this budget better have now been measured — a learned\npolicy, capping the searches, and removing the worst ratio — and all three lose\ntasks. The one that worked, `MAX_SLICE`, worked because it was applied to\nfamilies that provably could not use more time, not to families that merely\nlooked unproductive.\n\n## 4i. The two engines agree\n\nDevelopment split, 236 tasks, both engines at the same budget with the same\nscorer. Python solves 117, JavaScript 118, and they agree on 225 of the 236\ntasks — 95.3%.\n\nExact agreement is not the bar and could not be: both engines are wall-clock\nbounded and node is faster than CPython, so a family that finishes inside its\nslice on one can time out on the other. The number that would indicate a\nporting error is the count of **one-sided families**, where a family fires on\none side and never on the other, and it is zero. The visible asymmetry is\n`compose` (13 Python, 6 JavaScript) against `cellwise` (23, 31) — the faster\nruntime lets the cell rules finish and win the vote where the slower one leaves\nthat to `compose`. That is a scheduling difference between machines, and it is\nthe same scheduling story as §4d and §4f.\n\nThe feature banks themselves are compared exactly rather than statistically:\n`bench/dump_features.py` against `c4-arc/feature-parity.js`, two grids chosen\nto exercise every branch, 136 features a cell, zero mismatches.\n\n## 5. How to reproduce every number\n\nNothing below reads a test answer inside inference; the harness holds the\nanswers and compares afterwards.\n\n```sh\ncd astron/xyz\n\n# the incumbent, and the engine after this pass, on identical files\npython3 bench/run_arc.py --out evidence/V2_BASE.json  --budget 20 --jobs 4 \\\n    --planner policy/arc_planner.json --rank legacy --drop celltree,canvastree,typed\npython3 bench/run_arc.py --out evidence/V2_AFTER.json --budget 20 --jobs 4 \\\n    --planner policy/arc_planner.json\n\n# the three families added after that pass, on the same files and budget\npython3 bench/run_arc.py --out evidence/V3_GROW.json --budget 20 --jobs 4 \\\n    --planner policy/arc_planner.json\n\n# what each new family reaches on its own, against the tasks the engine\n# still misses -- fit rate and test-correct rate, no portfolio involved\npython3 bench/family_probe.py --family growtree,objtree,repeat \\\n    --against evidence/V2_AFTER.json\n\n# the R0/R1/R2/R3 ranking ladder and the reach ablation, one part at a time\npython3 bench/ablate.py --tasks data/split_hold --budget 20 --jobs 4 \\\n    --only R0_legacy_rank,R1_canonical_mdl,R2_version_space,\\\nR3_version_space_predictive,no_cell_tree,no_canvas_tree,no_typed_synthesis,\\\nno_tree_families,full\n\n# the V3 cross-fitted round; rejection leaves the incumbent untouched\npython3 bench/evolve.py --cross-fit 4 --budget 20 --jobs 4 --adopt policy/policy.json\n\n# the V3 planner with soft verified targets\npython3 bench/train_planner.py --soft --kappa 0.5 --eta 0.5 \\\n    --reference policy/arc_planner.json --out policy/arc_planner_soft.json\n\n# JavaScript parity\nsh ../../c4-arc/build.sh\nnode ../../c4-arc/bench.js --budget 20 --jobs 4 --out ../../c4-arc/JS_dev.json \\\n    --tasks data/split_dev\npython3 bench/conformance.py evidence/V2_AFTER.json ../../c4-arc/JS_dev.json\n\n# the results page, generated from the evidence rather than typed\npython3 bench/v2v3_report.py --base evidence/V2_BASE.json \\\n    --after evidence/V2_AFTER.json --out RESULTS-V2-V3.md\n```\n\nTests:\n\n```sh\npython3 -m unittest discover -s tests -q\n```\n\n## 4b. The one hyperparameter that was fitted\n\n`celltree.BASE_COST` and `canvastree.BASE_COST` are the cost a fitted tree\ncarries before its description length. The value matters only on *contested*\ntasks: where no specialist explains the demonstrations at all the tree is the\nonly candidate and its cost is irrelevant, but where a specialist already had\nthe answer, a two-split tree at cost one can outrank it.\n\nSo it was fitted on exactly those tasks — the 30 on which the incumbent and\nthis engine disagree, 24 the new families win and 6 they lose — and nowhere\nelse:\n\n| base cost | top 1 | top 2 |\n|---:|---:|---:|\n| 0.0 | 24 | 27 |\n| 0.5 | 24 | 27 |\n| **1.0** | **24** | **29** |\n| 2.5 | 23 | 29 |\n| 4.0 | 22 | 29 |\n\nMaking the family *more* conservative loses more of the tasks it wins than it\nrecovers of the tasks it loses; making it cheaper costs top-2 coverage. One was\nthe best value and it was already the value in use, which is a weaker result\nthan finding a better one but is the honest outcome. It is an environment\nvariable (`ASTRA_TREE_COST`) so the sweep is one command.\n\n## 5b. One design question left open\n\n`celltree` reports itself as the `cellwise` family and `canvastree` as\n`tiling`. The portfolio's vote takes, per family, the best score among that\nfamily's hypotheses and then combines *across* families, so two mechanisms\nsharing a family name cannot reinforce each other's answer the way two\ndifferent families can. Giving each its own family would let them vote\nindependently — and would also let a wrong answer from one of them gain\nindependent support, and would change their solver priors and any learned\nfamily skip. It was left alone because every task these families newly solve\nis solved by them alone, where the grouping cannot matter; if a later\nmeasurement shows contested tasks being lost, this is the first thing to try.\n\n## 6. A note on what \"held out\" means here\n\nThe shipped `data/split_hold` was written by the id-based split and no\ndevelopment decision was allowed to see it *in the previous pass*. That is not\ntrue of this one, and saying otherwise would be the easiest way to overstate\nwhat follows: the diagnosis in §1 was computed over all 550 task files, and\nwhile designing the cell-rule feature bank a handful of individual unsolved\ntasks were inspected directly — `arc1_d23f8c26`, `arc1_99fa7670`,\n`arc1_d406998b` and a few others, all from the ARC-AGI-1 *training* corpus.\nThe features that came out of that inspection are general (position relative\nto the centre; the last mark in a quadrant in scan order; ordinal rank among a\nrow's marks), and none of them names a task. But the corpus as a whole acted as\nthe development set for this pass, so every number here is a development\nnumber, and a sealed evaluation remains the only thing that would settle it.\n\n## 7. Measured results\n\nSee `RESULTS-V2-V3.md`, which is generated from the evidence files rather than\ntyped, so it and the runs behind it cannot drift apart.\n\nThe headline, against the incumbent on identical files at an identical budget:\n\n| corpus | tasks | incumbent | now | change |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| ARC-AGI-1 top 1 | 400 | 203 (50.75%) | **226 (56.50%)** | +23 |\n| ARC-AGI-1 top 2 | 400 | 212 (53.00%) | **240 (60.00%)** | +28 |\n| ARC-AGI-2 top 1 | 150 | 51 (34.00%) | **55 (36.67%)** | +4 |\n| all 550 top 1 | 550 | 254 (46.18%) | **281 (51.09%)** | +27 |\n\n32 newly solved, 5 regressed, 0 solver errors, one-sided sign test\np = 3.71e-06.\n\n**The 70% target is not reached.** ARC-AGI-1 is at 56.50% on one attempt and\n60.00% on two, which is +5.75 and +7.00 points on the incumbent, not +20. The\nlast pass of work on this page moved it 224 -> 226 and 238 -> 240, and the\nhonest summary of that pass is that two of its three new families were\nmeasured and stood down, one was kept, and a guard I invented for the kept one\nwas measured and reverted. Everything in §4j through §4m is that arithmetic in\nfull.\n\nThe evidence chain, in the order it was produced:\n\n| file | configuration |\n|---|---|\n| `evidence/V2_BASE.json` | the incumbent |\n| `evidence/V2_AFTER.json` | the previous pass |\n| `evidence/V3_GROW.json` | + diagonal coordinates, + `growtree` at 1.6 s |\n| `evidence/V3_FINAL.json` | + `objtree`, `growtree` trimmed, `repeat` stood down |\n| `evidence/V3_NOGROW.json` | the `--drop growtree` ablation |\n| `evidence/V3_BEST.json` | + the thin-evidence charge (reverted after this) |\n| `evidence/V3_SHIP.json` | **what is committed** |\n| `evidence/FAMILY_PROBE.json` | each family alone, against what the engine misses |\n"}, {"name": "ARCHITECTURE.md", "url": "/astron/xyz/ARCHITECTURE.md", "text": "# ASTRA — architecture\n\n> This is the original design document, retained for context. The improved\n> edition changes synthesis, ranking, movement reasoning, and validation as\n> described in `IMPROVEMENTS.md`. In particular, observational pruning now\n> retains a cost/depth Pareto frontier; leave-one-out credit follows a concrete\n> rule and its predictions; compatible shape laws remain alternatives. Any\n> numerical performance claims below refer to the supplied revision.\n\nASTRA is an ARC-AGI reasoning engine. It reads a task's demonstration pairs,\nsearches a space of *explanations*, ranks them by how much they compress the\nevidence, and applies the winner to the held-out input. It contains no neural\nnetwork and calls no external model. Every number in `RESULTS.md` was produced\nby the code in this directory, on this machine.\n\nThis document is about *why* the engine is shaped this way. The short version:\nARC is not one problem, and a single search space large enough to contain every\nARC rule is too large to search. The engine's answer is a portfolio of narrow,\nindependently-derived hypothesis generators, unified by one honest ranking rule\nand one general synthesiser that catches what the specialists miss — plus a\nlearning layer that reallocates search effort and grows the DSL from the tasks\nit has already solved.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The central problem: a search space you cannot afford to enumerate\n\nA DSL rich enough to express ARC solutions is combinatorially explosive. If the\nlibrary has `k` operators and solutions are `d` operators deep, naive\nenumeration is `k^d`. With `k ≈ 90` and `d = 4` that is 65 million programs, and\n`d = 4` is *not* deep enough for most of ARC.\n\nThere are only three ways out, and the engine uses all three.\n\n**(a) Collapse behaviourally identical programs.** The enumerator\n(`engine/enum_core.py`) never compares programs; it compares the *tuple of grids\na program produces on the task's own inputs*. Two syntactically different\nprograms with the same behaviour on the evidence are the same hypothesis, and\nonly one survives. `rot180` and `flip_h ∘ flip_v` are one node, not two. On real\ntasks this prunes each level by roughly an order of magnitude, and it is what\nmakes depth 4 tractable in pure Python.\n\n**(b) Replace search with fitting wherever a family is closed-form.** If a task\nis \"recolour every object according to some property of that object\", you do not\nhave to *search* for the recolouring — you can read it off the training pairs\nand check it for consistency. Most of the portfolio works this way: it fits, it\ndoes not enumerate. `objects_map`, `blocks`, `substitute`, `cellwise` and\n`symmetry` all solve a fitting problem in time linear in the data, over a space\nthat enumeration would need exponential time to cover.\n\n**(c) Let the space grow only where it has paid off before.** The learning layer\n(§5) mines abstractions from solved programs and installs them as single\noperators, so effective depth grows with experience instead of with the budget.\n\n## 2. Representation: the object vocabulary is the real bottleneck\n\nWhatever a solver can express is bounded by how the grid was carved up. ARC's\nnotion of \"object\" is not fixed — sometimes it is a 4-connected monochrome blob,\nsometimes an 8-connected multicoloured assembly, sometimes every cell of one\ncolour wherever it appears, sometimes a *dashed* structure whose parts are not\nadjacent at all. Committing to one segmentation loses tasks that need another,\nso `engine/objects.py` exposes seven and the portfolio tries each:\n\n| name | what it means |\n|---|---|\n| `c4`, `c8` | connected, single colour, 4- or 8-neighbourhood |\n| `m4`, `m8` | connected, any colours (multi-coloured assemblies) |\n| `color` | one object per colour, connected or not |\n| `g2`, `g2m` | connected up to a *gap* of 2 cells — dashed lines, scattered marks |\n| `cells` | every cell its own object |\n\nA second, subtler choice: **what counts as background**. Segmenting against the\ntask background finds the objects; segmenting against a *wall* colour instead\nfinds the regions those walls enclose. \"Colour each pen by how big it is\" is a\nregion task, not an object task, and it is invisible unless you are willing to\ncall the walls background. `objects_map` therefore tries the top few colours as\nbackground, not just the inferred one.\n\nGrids are immutable tuples of tuples. That is load-bearing: it makes them\nhashable, which is what allows observational-equivalence pruning, the\nsegmentation cache, the feature cache and the window-scan cache to exist at all.\nThose caches are not micro-optimisations — before they were added, a single task\nrecomputed the same connected-components analysis several hundred times and the\nengine spent most of its budget on it.\n\n## 3. The portfolio: eighteen ways to be right\n\nEach module in `engine/solvers/` exports one function, `generate(ctx) -> [Hyp]`.\nA `Hyp` is a *total function from an input grid to an output grid* plus a\nstructural cost. Modules do not decide anything and never see a test answer;\nthey propose.\n\n| module | the family it explains |\n|---|---|\n| `geometry` | dihedral maps, crops, halves, quadrants, tilings, fractal self-stamping, gravity, hole filling, union with a mirror image |\n| `colormap` | recolourings that generalise: by frequency *rank*, by role, by relation — not by literal colour |\n| `partition` | separator lines and even splits into panels; select a panel relationally, or combine all panels cellwise (the AND/OR/XOR family) |\n| `blocks` | learned dictionaries: cell → patch, patch → cell, panel → panel |\n| `symmetry` | occlusion repair by symmetry-group closure (§4) |\n| `tiling` | periodic motif extraction, periodic and *diagonal* fill, extension |\n| `select` / `regions` | the answer is one object or one region: window scanning, frames, marked rectangles, colour answers |\n| `cellwise` | local-rule induction over 24 context families (§4) |\n| `objects_map` | per-object edits as a decision function over object features |\n| `substitute` | shape → stencil dictionaries (\"each seed grows into this motif\") |\n| `sequence` / `paint` | motion and drawing: gravity with collision, rays, connections, line extension, rectangle completion, reflection across a separator, symmetrisation |\n| `analogy` | one complete exemplar in the grid, fragments elsewhere; complete them |\n| `compose` | shallow enumeration (depth 2) so short programs are found early and ranked cheaply |\n| `rewrite` | re-pose the task (crop it, compress it, downscale the outputs) and hand it back to the specialists |\n| `cascade` | two-stage solving (§6) |\n| `enumerate` | depth-4 bottom-up synthesis with a binary combination layer — the general fallback |\n\nThe portfolio is not a pile of special cases; it is a set of *different\ninductive biases*. Their independence is what makes the ensemble vote in §7\nmeaningful.\n\n## 4. Two mechanisms worth explaining in full\n\n### Symmetry repair by group closure\n\nThe task: a structured grid has a region blanked out, and the answer is the\nrepaired grid (or just the missing patch).\n\nThe naive approach — test the eight dihedral symmetries — fails immediately,\nbecause real ARC symmetries are about *arbitrary axes*, not the grid centre, and\nare often translational or diagonal rather than reflective.\n\nSo `engine/solvers/symmetry.py` collects a large candidate set of cell\npermutations: mirrors about every half-integer horizontal and vertical axis,\ntranslations by every row and column period, translations along both diagonals,\nand (on square grids) the two diagonal reflections and a quarter turn. A\npermutation is kept only if it never contradicts an *observed* pair of cells and\nis supported by enough overlap to be more than a coincidence.\n\nThe surviving permutations are then fed to a union-find over cells. This is the\nkey step: union-find closes under composition, so unioning under a set of\ngenerators recovers the *entire group they generate* without ever enumerating\nit. If the grid is invariant under a horizontal mirror and a vertical\ntranslation, the engine gets every glide reflection for free. Each orbit that\ncontains at least one observed cell is then determined, and any orbit that is\nentirely unobserved makes the strict variant decline to answer rather than\nguess.\n\nAdding diagonal translations to that generator set was worth real tasks on its\nown: a grid whose colour depends on `(r+c) mod k` has no row period and no\ncolumn period, so without them it is not merely hard to repair — it is\ninvisible.\n\n### Local-rule induction, and why it needs a capacity guard\n\nFor same-shape tasks, `cellwise` tries to explain each output cell as a function\nof a bounded local context of the input: the cell's colour; its 4- or\n8-neighbourhood, ordered or as a multiset; a bitmask of which neighbours match\nit; counts of non-background neighbours; row/column parity and modular position;\ndistance to the border; the nearest non-background colour in each of the four\ndirections; the size, dimensions or shape of the object the cell belongs to; the\nfrequency rank of its colour; whether its row or column is uniform. Twenty-four\nfamilies in all. Each is a lookup table, fitted in one pass and rejected on the\nfirst contradiction.\n\nThis single module covers denoising, outlining, ray drawing, colour swaps,\nparity stripes, and a long tail of \"each pixel becomes…\" rules.\n\nIt is also the most dangerous module in the engine, because a rich enough\ncontext *always* fits. A 3×3-neighbourhood table with as many entries as there\nare cells has memorised the training data and predicts nothing. Two guards:\n\n1. a hard capacity bound — a table with more than a third as many entries as\n   observations is rejected outright;\n2. **internal leave-one-out**: refit the table with one training pair withheld\n   and check whether it still predicts that pair. A rule that survives is\n   discounted; a rule that fails is *penalised*, not merely un-rewarded.\n\nThe second guard is the one that matters. Before it existed, this module was\nproducing the top-ranked hypothesis on dozens of tasks it then got wrong.\n\n## 5. Self-improvement, and what would make it dishonest\n\nThe engine improves itself along three axes, all offline, all local. The design\nconstraint throughout: **a self-improving system that grades its own homework\nwill drift**, so nothing is adopted without a paired test against the version it\nreplaces.\n\n**Signature-conditioned priors.** Each task is reduced to a handful of\ncategorical signatures — does the shape change, and how; does the palette grow\nor shrink; is there a separator colour; how large is the grid; how many colours;\nare the inputs symmetric. From the record of which family produced the accepted\nprogram, `engine/learn.py` fits a bias per (signature, family) as a log-odds\nagainst a uniform prior. At solve time the biases of the task's own signatures\nare summed into the ranking prior. This changes *both* what is believed first\nand, through module ordering, where the time goes. The bias is clamped to ±3, so\nexperience can reorder families but can never silence one.\n\n**Library learning.** Accepted enumerator programs are parsed back into operator\nchains; frequent contiguous sub-chains are compiled into single named operators\nand installed into the DSL. This is the mechanism that makes the search space\ngrow with experience rather than stay fixed: a depth-4 search over a library\ncontaining 3-operator abstractions reaches 12-operator programs. The abstraction\nis a closure over base operators only, so abstractions cannot nest into each\nother uncontrollably.\n\n**Operator bias.** Operators that appear in accepted programs sort earlier in\nenumeration. This matters more than it sounds: the enumerator is truncated by a\nstate cap, so what is explored first is what is explored *at all*.\n\n**The gate.** `bench/evolve.py` runs the loop. Each round: run the fit split\nunder the policy in force; fit a candidate from accumulated experience; re-run\nthe fit split under the candidate; compare *per task*. Adoption requires a\none-sided sign test over discordant tasks,\n\n```\np = Σ_{i≥w} C(w+l, i) / 2^(w+l)\n```\n\nwhere `w` is tasks the candidate solves and the incumbent does not and `l` the\nreverse. A candidate that merely reshuffles which tasks it solves does not pass.\nThe holdout split — chosen by MD5 of the task id, so it is reproducible and\nindependent of solve order — never fits anything and never gates anything; it is\nmeasured once, at the end, to report whether the policy transfers.\n\nThis is a real but bounded claim. It is policy learning over a fixed hypothesis\nspace plus a compression-based extension of that space. It is not open-ended\nself-modification, and a single sign test does not control error over repeated\nadaptive experiments — which is exactly why the holdout exists and why the\nlineage of accepted and *rejected* rounds is recorded in `policy/lineage.jsonl`.\n\n## 6. Two-stage solving: using the near misses\n\nEvery solver in the portfolio is all-or-nothing — a hypothesis either reproduces\nevery training output or it is discarded. That throws away the most informative\nsignal available: the transform that gets the grid *almost* right.\n\n\"Crop to the frame, then recolour by size\" is invisible to every single-stage\nfamily and trivial once the crop has been applied. So `cascade` runs a shallow\nsearch scored by **cell agreement** instead of exact match, keeps the transforms\nthat make the most progress (preferring those that get the *shape* right, since\na second stage can repaint cells but cannot resize the grid), re-poses each as a\nfresh task `(stage1(input), output)`, and hands it to the specialists. The\ncomposition is validated end-to-end like anything else, so a first stage that\nonly looked promising costs nothing when the second stage fails to close the gap.\n\n`rewrite` is the same idea applied in the other direction: rewrite the task\nitself — crop it, strip its scaffolding lines, downscale its outputs — and reuse\nthe entire specialist portfolio on the rewritten version, inverting the rewrite\non the way out.\n\n## 7. Ranking: a description-length argument, then a vote\n\nFitting the training pairs is necessary and nowhere near sufficient. On a\ntypical task the portfolio produces several hypotheses that all reproduce the\ndemonstrations and disagree about the answer. Choosing between them *is* the\nproblem.\n\nThe score is\n\n```\nscore = family_prior + structural_cost + capacity_penalty − LOO_evidence\n```\n\n`structural_cost` is the size of the program or table the hypothesis carries.\n`capacity_penalty` grows with the number of free parameters it fitted — a lookup\ntable pays for every row. `LOO_evidence` refits the *generating family* with one\ntraining pair withheld and asks whether it still predicts that pair; surviving\nearns up to −3, failing costs +1.5, and too few pairs to judge costs nothing.\nThis is Occam with the arithmetic made explicit: the shortest rule that still\nexplains a pair it never saw is the one to trust.\n\nThen the vote. Independent solver families that arrive at the *same answer* are\nmuch stronger evidence than one family agreeing with itself, so each answer is\nscored by `Σ_families exp(−best_score_in_that_family / 2)` — each family\ncontributes once, at its best score, no matter how many variants it emitted.\nThis stops a module that generates nine hundred hypotheses from outvoting one\nthat generates three.\n\n## 8. Measurement, and the guarantees behind it\n\nA solver is constructed from the training pairs and the test *inputs*. Test\noutputs never enter the solver's view — they stay in the harness and are\ncompared afterwards. This is structural, not a convention: `engine/task.py`'s\n`Ctx` has no field to put them in.\n\nA task counts as solved only when **every** test pair of that task is exact.\nPartial cell agreement is recorded but is diagnostic only and is never scored.\nBoth top-1 (one attempt) and top-2 (the two attempts official ARC-AGI allows)\nare reported. Per-task wall-clock is bounded by the orchestrator's deadline;\nsolver exceptions are caught and counted as unsolved rather than excluded.\n\n`bench/compare.py` compares against the previous engine per task — the same 550\nfiles, the same identifiers, a win/loss table and a sign test — not two aggregate\nrates.\n\n## 9. Where the budget goes\n\nTime is allocated in two phases. The specialists are cheap and either fire or do\nnot, so they share the first 45% of the budget; whatever they leave unspent\nflows to the search modules — `compose`, `rewrite`, `cascade`, `enumerate` —\nwhich can always use more. Within phase 2 each module takes a share of what\nactually remains, computed as it starts rather than fixed in advance.\n\n## 10. What this engine cannot do\n\nStated plainly, because the honest limits are part of the result.\n\n- It has **no notion of physics or agents**. Tasks whose rule is \"simulate this\n  until it stops\" are outside every family here. *Counting is now partially\n  covered*: `engine/solvers/counting.py` fits the family where the answer's\n  size or content is a function of a count read off the input -- an `n x n`\n  block, a bar of length `n`, the input tiled or scaled `n` times. It fits, it\n  does not search, and a counter that is constant across the training inputs is\n  penalised because it cannot be told apart from a constant. Tasks whose rule is\n  \"however many steps it takes\" remain out of range.\n- Its compositional reach is **two stages**, plus whatever depth the enumerator\n  and learned abstractions provide. Genuinely three- and four-stage ARC-2 tasks\n  are largely out of range, which is most of why the ARC-2 rate is roughly half\n  the ARC-1 rate.\n- The learning layer improves *allocation and reach* within a hypothesis space\n  that a person designed. It does not invent a new kind of explanation. It\n  cannot, and the architecture does not pretend otherwise.\n- These are public development sets. Public ARC training tasks informed which\n  families were worth writing, and the fit split participates in policy\n  selection. The holdout number is the transfer estimate; nothing here is an\n  official hidden-test result, and it should not be read as one.\n"}, {"name": "HANDOFF-GPT6.md", "url": "/astron/xyz/HANDOFF-GPT6.md", "text": "# Handoff: what would actually help\n\nWritten for a second agent working in parallel on this checkout. It says where\nthe engine is, what has already been measured (including the things that did\n**not** work, so they are not tried again), and four pieces of work that do not\ncollide with what I am holding.\n\nBranch: `claude/magical-noether-2izbc9`. Everything below is reproducible with\nthe commands in `ARC-V2-V3.md` §5.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Where the numbers are\n\nFull 550 public task files, 20-second per-task budget, `policy/arc_planner.json`,\ntwo attempts scored, four workers.\n\n| | incumbent | now |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| ARC-AGI-1 top 1 | 203 (50.75%) | **226 (56.50%)** |\n| ARC-AGI-1 top 2 | 212 (53.00%) | **240 (60.00%)** |\n| ARC-AGI-2 top 1 | 51 | **55** |\n| all 550 top 1 | 254 | **281** |\n\n32 newly solved, 5 regressed, 0 solver errors, one-sided sign test p = 3.7e-6.\nFour of the five regressions keep the answer at rank 2, so at ARC's two-attempt\nscoring one is a real loss.\n\nCandidate coverage is the number the work was aimed at: tasks where nothing fit\nthe demonstrations at all went from **212 to 134**.\n\nA later pass added three families and kept one. `objtree` — per-object edits as\na decision tree, with actions coded so that a copy leaf means \"recolour to the\ncolour of the thing containing it\" — is in the portfolio and worth two tasks.\n`growtree` (cell rules iterated to a fixed point) and `repeat` (motifs marching\nto the edge with a derived step) are **not**: both are measured negatives,\ndocumented in their own module docstrings and in `ARC-V2-V3.md` §4j–§4m. Five\ndiagonal coordinates in `celltree` are worth two more tasks. Read\n`bench/family_probe.py` before proposing a family — it answers \"what does this\nreach on its own\" in one command, and it would have retired §4.1 in a minute\nrather than a day.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Do not redo these. They are measured, and they are negative.\n\nEach of these cost a full corpus run to establish. Re-deriving them is the most\nlikely way to waste a day.\n\n**The V2 version-space ranker is worth zero tasks.** R0/R1/R2/R3 on the 314\nheld-out tasks: 158, 159, 158, 158. The equation is implemented exactly —\nKraft-tight code lengths, bounded refits, capped cross-subset regret, every\nsanity check in §16 of the specification has a test — and it does not move the\nscore, because ranking was never the bottleneck. `bench/diagnose.py` found\nexactly one development task where the right answer was generated and ranked\nbelow position two. Keep it, do not expect it to pay.\n\n**Compute is not the constraint.** 20 s → 45 s on the held-out split: 158 → 160.\nMore than doubling the budget buys two tasks.\n\n**The library objective correctly adopts nothing here.** `J_lib` on the\ndevelopment half refuses every candidate macro. The most reused fragment,\n`recolor_by(pick_crop(...))`, occurs in three content groups and saves 8.7 bits\nan occurrence against a 13.7-bit library cost — but those tasks already have\n18-bit explanations and the fragment only appears in their 58-bit alternatives,\nwhich also carry `R = 6.00`, the capped maximum regret. `Z_t` is dominated by\nits cheapest term. This is the §5 guard working. It is not a broken feature.\n\n**Raising the tree families' base cost loses more than it recovers.** Swept on\nthe 30 tasks where the incumbent and this engine disagree: cost 0.0 → 24 top 1 /\n27 top 2, 1.0 → 24 / 29, 2.5 → 23 / 29, 4.0 → 22 / 29. One was already the value\nin use.\n\n**A learned policy transfers negatively.** Fitted from the development half\n(32 signature biases, 20 skips, no macros) and measured on the held-out 314:\n161 top 1 without it, 156 with. Zero wins, five losses; the V3 gate rejects it.\nThe skips are the crude part -- a family with no recorded solve for a signature\nis dropped entirely rather than de-prioritised.\n\n**Removing the worst time-to-fits ratio loses tasks.** `objchain` takes 7.9% of\nthe clock at 0.17 fits per second; dropping it is 161 -> 158, zero wins, three\nlosses. A generator that fits rarely is the one that has an idea when nothing\nelse does. Read `bench/module_cost.py` before assuming a family is wasteful.\n\n**Two feature groups bought nothing**: seed-displacement (\"draw\" bank) and a\nseparate strided-read bank. Both are kept — the features are cheap and the draw\nbank is what its category needs — but neither has paid yet.\n\n**Per-object stencil tables do not generalise. This retires §4.1 below.**\nMeasured offline on the 119 unsolved same-shape ARC-1 tasks: a table from an\nobject key to the patch it becomes, over five segmentations, six keys (colour,\nnormalised shape, size, shape+colour, the raw patch, and one global key) and\nmargins 0 to 3. Fifty-one are *realisable* at margin 1 — the table exists and\nreproduces every demonstration — and nine survive the held-out refit. Those\nnine solve **zero** test grids. The failure is structural, not tuning: a\nshape-keyed table memorises, and the test grid's key is one it has never seen.\nThese tasks need a *transformation* per object, not a lookup.\n\n**A bigger tree is not the answer either.** With a 512-leaf budget and the\nheld-out refit switched off, `celltree` fits the demonstrations of 39 of those\n119 and gets **zero** test grids right. The leaf budget is not what holds the\nfamily back; the features are.\n\n**Symmetry repair adds nothing here.** A standalone repairer — every mirror\naxis at half-cell resolution, both diagonals, every row and column period,\nclosed under composition, with the hole colour swept over all ten — fits\n**zero** of the 162 unsolved ARC-1 tasks. `symmetry.py` already has the ones\nthis shape of rule can reach.\n\n---\n\n## 3. What did work, so you know the shape of a win\n\nEvery large gain came from *spending less*, not from seeing more:\n\n- **Delta targets.** The cell tree predicts the difference from the input, with\n  one `KEEP` leaf covering every untouched cell. 13 correct → from 8. Bigger\n  than every feature added before or after it.\n- **Copy leaves.** A leaf may return a colour-valued feature's value instead of\n  a constant. \"Every mark paints to the right edge\" needs the leaf to emit *the\n  colour of the mark on this row*, which is not a constant at all. 16 → 20.\n- **`MAX_SLICE`.** A generator declares the wall-clock beyond which it has\n  nothing further to offer; the remainder falls through. At 20 s this reaches\n  the 45-second solve rate without the 45 seconds.\n\nIf a change adds capacity, expect it to be neutral. If it removes capacity the\ntask does not need, expect it to win.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Four things that would help, in order\n\nThese do not touch what I am holding (`engine/solvers/celltree.py`,\n`engine/solvers/paneltree.py`, `engine/portfolio.py` scheduling, `bench/`).\n\n### 4.1 ~~Per-object stencils~~ — RETIRED; the measurement is in §2\n\n**Do not build this.** It was measured, offline, on the 119 unsolved same-shape\nARC-1 tasks: the table is realisable on 51 of them, survives the held-out refit\non 9, and solves **0**. A shape-keyed table memorises and the test grid's key\nis one it has never seen. The category is still the largest one -- 66 of the\n162 unsolved ARC-1 tasks are \"the input plus something drawn on the\nbackground\" -- but a stencil table is not the way in. What follows is the\noriginal text, kept so the reasoning that led to it is visible.\n\n~~The cell tree reaches the ones where the stamp is a fixed offset pattern; it\ndoes not reach the ones where the stamp depends on the seed (its colour, its\nshape, its size) or extends past the neighbourhood.~~ `engine/solvers/substitute.py`\nalready learns \"each seed grows into this motif\" for one stencil. What is\nmissing is a *table*: seed key → stencil, with the key drawn from the same\nobject-feature vocabulary the cell tree uses (colour, shape rank, size, hole\ncount), and the stencil read off the demonstrations rather than enumerated.\n\nOwn `engine/solvers/substitute.py` and a new module if you need one. Do not\nedit `celltree.py`.\n\n### 4.2 The shrinking tasks nobody has looked at\n\n35 unsolved tasks shrink the grid. Only 3 of them are \"the output is a window of\nthe input\" — `locate.py` already has those. The other 32 construct something,\nand no one has categorised them. That is an afternoon of reading tasks and it\nwould tell us whether there is a family there or a long tail.\n\n### 4.3 ARC-AGI-2\n\n54/150. Essentially untouched this pass; every diagnostic above was computed on\nARC-AGI-1. The same categorisation run on the 96 unsolved ARC-AGI-2 tasks may\nwell point somewhere completely different.\n\n### 4.4 Run the V3 cross-fitted loop for real\n\n`python3 bench/evolve.py --cross-fit 4 --budget 20 --jobs 4 --adopt policy/policy.json`\n\nIt is implemented, tested on a 12-task corpus, and has never been run on the\nreal one because it costs eight full-corpus runs (~3 hours). It may adopt\nnothing — see §2 — but \"we ran it and the gate refused\" is a result and\n\"we never ran it\" is not. A policy fitted from the development half alone is\nalready at `policy/policy_v3_dev.json` (32 signature biases, 20 skips, no\nmacros) and has never been evaluated on the held-out half; that is one run.\n\n---\n\n## 4.5 The JavaScript port is behind by two solver families\n\n`delta_stencils` and `panel_exemplars` exist in Python only. Verified against\nthe browser bundle: it solves `arc1_3bd67248`, `arc1_776ffc46` and\n`arc1_b27ca6d3` -- everything this pass won through `celltree` and `objtree` --\nand fails `arc1_29c11459`, which is a `subst_delta_*` win.\n\nThat matters beyond tidiness. `c4-mini.html` loads `c4-arc-engine.js`, so the\npublic page runs the JavaScript engine and not the Python one; a family that\nexists on one side only is a family the page does not have. It will also show\nup in `bench/conformance.py` as a one-sided solver family, which until now has\nbeen zero and is the check that catches exactly this.\n\nPorting them is bounded work and the Python is the specification.\n\n## 5. Rules that are not negotiable\n\n- **No external model, no API, no network.** The engine is pure symbolic Python\n  and JavaScript and must stay that way. There is no key to add and adding one\n  would invalidate every number here.\n- **Never hand-edit `c4-arc-engine.js`.** Edit `c4-arc/src/*.js` and run\n  `sh c4-arc/build.sh`. The sources share one lexical scope and concatenate in\n  filename order, so check dependency order before naming a new file.\n- **Keep the two ports in step.** `python3 bench/dump_features.py > /tmp/py.json`\n  then `node c4-arc/feature-parity.js /tmp/py.json` must report zero mismatches.\n  It has already caught four drifted feature names and a leaf-value bug that\n  only appeared for non-numeric labels.\n- **No test output reaches inference or fitting.** `Ctx` carries test *inputs*;\n  the harness holds the answers and compares afterwards. Every refit is handed\n  `ctx.train` pairs only.\n- **Every claim needs a paired measurement** on identical task files at an\n  identical budget, and the evidence file committed. `bench/v2v3_report.py`\n  generates the results page from the evidence so the two cannot drift.\n- **Report negative results.** Three of the most useful findings in this pass\n  are things that did not work.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Interfaces you will need\n\n`engine/program.py` — typed programs as canonical structure `c` plus literals\n`theta`. `Hyp.prog` is the optional contract: a hypothesis that carries a real\n`Prog` takes part in version-space ranking, one that does not keeps legacy\nranking. Nothing infers parameters from a name.\n\n`engine/solvers/celltree.py` — 136 per-cell features in named banks, decision\ntrees with equality and threshold splits, delta targets, copy leaves, a\nsmallest-tree-first ladder, a held-out demonstration refit. `fit_pairs` is\npublic so other solvers can fit a tree from their own pairs; `refine` uses it to\nfinish a specialist's near miss.\n\n`MAX_SLICE` on a solver module — seconds beyond which it has nothing to offer.\nDeclare it on any generator whose work is a finite enumeration. Do not declare\nit on a search.\n\n`bench/module_cost.py` — where the budget actually goes, per generator, from a\nsingle benchmark record. Use it before assuming a family is expensive.\n\n`bench/conformance.py` — Python versus JavaScript agreement, and which solver\nfamilies fire on one side and never the other.\n"}, {"name": "README.GABRIEL.md", "url": "/astron/xyz/README.GABRIEL.md", "text": "# GABRIEL\n\n**GABRIEL** is the system: the ASTRA symbolic ARC engine with a language model\nstrapped to the places where it has to make a choice.  ASTRA is the engine\ninside it -- the package names, imports and benchmark scripts keep that name so\nnothing breaks; GABRIEL is the thing as a whole.\n\nThe model is trained here, from scratch, in pure Python, on programs this\nengine wrote.  **No external model is consulted at any point.**  There is no API\nkey, no network call, no vendor SDK, and no dependency outside the standard\nlibrary.  That is not a stylistic choice: an ARC result produced with help from\nan outside model is not an ARC result.\n\n```bash\npython3 -m gabriel data/arc/arc1_007bbfb7.json --budget 20 --out preds.json\npython3 -m gabriel.train --max-seconds 120          # one gated training pass\npython3 -m gabriel.bench --mode both --limit 32     # paired A/B against stock\n```\n\n## The model\n\nA sparse log-linear (maximum-entropy) autoregressive language model over the\nengine's own program language.\n\nThe engine names every hypothesis it builds, and those names are a small\nregular language: `crop(rot90($))`, `T@fractal#0`, `sep#5.and->2`,\n`paint#1($)>>c4@0.by_size_rank`.  `gabriel/tokens.py` scans that into tokens.\nA training sequence is a task *prompt* followed by a program:\n\n```\nprompt:  <sig>shape:diff  <sig>size:up  <sig>ratio:3x3 ...\nprogram: <fam>geometry  fractal  #  0  <eos>\n```\n\nThe signatures come from `engine.learn.signatures`, computed from training\npairs only.  The solver family is emitted as the first token, which is why\n`P(family | task)` is something the model produces directly rather than a\nsecond head bolted on the side.\n\n```\nP(t | h) = softmax_t  sum_{f in F(h)} w[f][t]\n\nF(h) = { b,  1|prev,  2|prev2|prev,  s|sig (one per signature),\n         f|family,  p|position }\n```\n\nTrained by SGD with AdaGrad on cross-entropy, with a sampled softmax\n(negatives from the unigram raised to 0.75) so a pass fits inside a cron tick,\nand early-stopped on a held-out slice.  Perplexity is always reported with the\n**exact** softmax, never the sampled approximation.\n\nMeasured on this checkout:\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| corpus | 921 training / 201 dev examples, from 316 fit-split tasks |\n| vocabulary | 282 tokens |\n| parameters | 960 feature rows, ~118k non-zero weights (2.5 MB of JSON) |\n| dev perplexity | **12.9** |\n| unigram baseline, same dev, same vocabulary | 52.0 |\n| uniform baseline | 282.0 |\n| training time | ~7 s for a full pass on 4 vCPU |\n\n## Where it is strapped in\n\nAll three points use extension points `engine/learn.py` already defines.  **No\nfile under `engine/`, `bench/`, or `engine/solvers/` was modified.**\n\n**1. Which family to believe.**  `gabriel/bind.py` subclasses `learn.Policy`;\n`bias_for(sigs)` adds `-w log(P(family | task) * n)` to the fitted prior, in the\nsame units and with the same `[-3, 3]` clamp the fitted policy uses.  The\nportfolio ranks and orders solver modules with it.\n\n**2. Which operator to try next.**  `enum_core.search` subtracts an operator\nbias when it sorts its library and adds it to each node's beam bonus -- in a\nsearch truncated by a state cap, what is explored first is what is explored at\nall.  `GabrielPolicy.op_bias` is a property: `portfolio.solve` calls\n`bias_for(sigs)` and then reads `op_bias`, in that order, so the property\nanswers for *that* task.  Values are squashed into `[0, 1.2]`, measured against\nthe median operator, so the model can promote what it believes in and can never\nsilence anything.  If that call order ever changed, the property falls back to\nthe fitted static bias -- degraded, never wrong.\n\n**3. Which programs to write.**  `gabriel/proposer.py` is an ordinary solver\nmodule.  It decodes operator chains from the model under a grammar built from\n`enum_core.unary_ops(ctx)` -- so it can only propose programs that exist for\nthis task, learned abstractions included -- and hands them to the portfolio as\nhypotheses.  The portfolio then does what it always does: runs each one on\nevery training pair and discards it unless every pair matches.  The model\nproposes; the verifier disposes.  A confident model gets no benefit of the\ndoubt.\n\n## What it measures\n\nPaired A/B, 32 fit-split tasks, `--budget 8 --jobs 4`, scored by\n`bench/run_arc.py`'s own `score_predictions` (every test pair exact, top-1):\n\n| | solved | CPU seconds |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| stock engine | 16 / 32 | 204.4 |\n| GABRIEL bound | 16 / 32 | 167.3 |\n| paired | 0 wins, 0 losses, sign test p = 1.0 | **-18%** |\n\nRead that honestly: on this sample **binding the model is accuracy-neutral and\nabout 18% cheaper in CPU**.  It solves the same tasks sooner, because the\nfamily prior gets the right module to fire earlier -- it does not yet solve\ntasks the engine could not.  The chain corpus is the reason: only about 1,350\nof the ~21,000 program tokens in `evidence/` are enumerator chains, which is\nthin data for the proposer.  `gabriel/harvest.py` exists to grow exactly that,\none cron tick at a time.\n\nNothing here changes the numbers in `RESULTS.md`.  Those are the stock engine\non the bundled public files: ARC-1 168/400, ARC-2 41/150, 209/550 top-1.  They\nare not official and not a hidden-set result.\n\n## Discipline\n\nThe reason to trust a number from this loop is that adoption is gated the same\nway in both halves:\n\n* a candidate **policy** is adopted only if a one-sided sign test over\n  discordant tasks says it beat the incumbent (`bench/evolve.py`, untouched);\n* a candidate **language model** is adopted only if its perplexity fell on a\n  held-out dev slice it was never trained on (`gabriel/train.py`), and within\n  training, only the best-generalising epoch's weights are kept.  The two\n  models are compared over a common support, but each keeps its own idea of\n  which tokens it knows -- scoring an incumbent's unseen token as a bare zero\n  would rank it above every token that incumbent had learned to distrust, and\n  every candidate would look like an improvement.\n\nTwo further rules keep the corpus clean:\n\n* only fit-split tasks are ever read or harvested.  The evolve holdout is\n  never trained on and never gates anything, here either;\n* nothing reads a record's `solved` flag.  A program enters the corpus because\n  it reproduced every *training* pair -- whether it then turned out to be right\n  on the task's test pair changes nothing.  The model learns from information a\n  solver is allowed to have.\n\n`tests/test_gabriel.py` covers all of it -- 26 tests, including that decoding\ncannot leave the operator set, that a proposed program still has to fit, that\nthe holdout never enters the corpus, and that the solve context still has no\ntest outputs on it.\n\n```bash\npython3 -m unittest tests.test_gabriel -v\n```\n\n## Files\n\n```\ngabriel/tokens.py     the token language over program names\ngabriel/lm.py         the model: features, training, perplexity, decoding\ngabriel/corpus.py     evidence/*.json -> training examples (fit split only)\ngabriel/train.py      one gated training pass\ngabriel/bind.py       GabrielPolicy: the model as an engine policy\ngabriel/proposer.py   the model as a solver module\ngabriel/harvest.py    grow the corpus, one rotating slice per tick\ngabriel/bench.py      paired measurement, using run_arc's own scoring\ngabriel/__main__.py   inference CLI\n```\n\nOperations -- the VPS, the cron, the Namecheap terms -- are in\n[README.OPS.md](README.OPS.md).\n\n## The text sibling\n\n`text/` is a separate package that applies this architecture -- signatures, a\nconditional log-linear scorer, competing hypothesis families, constrained\ndecoding, verification before acceptance, a held-out gate -- to natural-language\nqueries instead of ARC programs.  It shares no vocabulary with GABRIEL and\nimports nothing from `gabriel/` or `engine/`; an ARC program vocabulary cannot\nscore English and pretending it can would be a fake integration.  It exports two\nsmall JSON artifacts that `robots.html` loads same-origin, which is the whole of\nthe connection -- ASTRON runs no daemon, so there is no API to call.\n\nNothing in this file changed to accommodate it.  See [text/README.md](text/README.md).\n"}, {"name": "README.OPS.md", "url": "/astron/xyz/README.OPS.md", "text": "# Running GABRIEL's training on a Namecheap VPS\n\nOne cron tick = one bounded training round, then the process exits.  There is\nno daemon, nothing listens on a port, and nothing outside this box is called.\n\n## Install\n\nTarget: a Namecheap **KVM VPS** (user-responsible plan), Ubuntu 24.04, root over\nSSH on port 22.  Quasar-class (4 vCPU / 6 GB) is comfortable; 2 vCPU / 2 GB\nworks, and the tick script drops its worker count on its own when it sees the\nsmaller box.  Namecheap **Shared/Stellar cPanel hosting is out of scope** --\nthere is no \"Setup Python App\" step here and nothing goes in `public_html`.\n\n```bash\nscp astraarcengineimproved.zip root@YOUR_VPS_IP:/root/\nssh root@YOUR_VPS_IP\ncd /root && unzip -q astraarcengineimproved.zip\ncd /root/astraarcengine-improved/astra\nbash scripts/install_namecheap.sh\n```\n\nThe installer is idempotent.  It installs `python3 python3-venv python3-pip\nunzip tmux`, creates `.venv`, creates `policy/ logs/ queue/ predictions/`,\nsmoke-tests the imports, and installs one crontab line -- replacing only its\nown, leaving the rest of your crontab alone.\n\nThe line it installs:\n\n```cron\n*/6 * * * * /root/astraarcengine-improved/astra/scripts/cron_evolve.sh\n```\n\nEvery six minutes as you asked.  `scripts/crontab.line` explains why the\ninterval is not what bounds the work: the tick takes `/tmp/astra-evolve.lock`\nwith `flock -n`, so a tick that finds a round already running logs one line and\nexits 0.  One evolve round takes roughly **20-60 minutes** on 4 vCPU, so most\nsix-minute ticks are deliberate no-ops and the schedule really means \"start the\nnext round as soon as the last one finishes\".  `*/15` is equally correct.\n\n## What one tick does\n\n```\n1. harvest   python3 -m gabriel.harvest --limit 24 --budget 8 --jobs $JOBS\n2. train     python3 -m gabriel.train --max-seconds 120 --quiet\n3. evolve    python3 bench/evolve.py --rounds 1 --budget 14 --jobs $JOBS\n```\n\nAll three run at `nice 10` so a web stack on the same VPS keeps its CPU, and\n`$JOBS` is `min(4, nproc)`, dropped to 2 under 3 GB of RAM and to 1 under\n1.5 GB.  The two short steps run first so a long evolve round cannot starve\nthem.  Skip either half with `ASTRA_EVOLVE=0` or `GABRIEL_TRAIN=0`.\n\n## Where the training lives\n\n| File | Written by | What it is |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `policy/policy.json` | `bench/evolve.py` | the symbolic policy in force: solver priors, operator bias, up to 24 mined abstractions |\n| `policy/gabriel_lm.json` | `gabriel/train.py` | the language model in force |\n| `policy/lineage.jsonl` | `bench/evolve.py` | rounds of the **most recent** evolve run only -- it is rewritten each run |\n| `logs/lineage.history.jsonl` | the tick script | every evolve round ever, appended after each tick |\n| `policy/gabriel_lineage.jsonl` | `gabriel/train.py` | every language-model round, appended, with the perplexity that decided it |\n| `evidence/evolution.json` | `bench/evolve.py` | the last evolve run's summary |\n| `evidence/gabriel_lm.json` | `gabriel/train.py` | the last training pass's summary |\n| `evidence/gabriel-harvest-*.json` | `gabriel/harvest.py` | run records, which are the model's training data; pruned to 40 files |\n\nThe tick script never deletes `policy/policy.json` or `policy/lineage.jsonl`.\nIt copies lineage rows out before the next run overwrites them, which is the\nonly reason `logs/lineage.history.jsonl` exists.\n\n## Confirming a round actually happened\n\n```bash\ncrontab -l                                   # the schedule\ntail -f logs/evolve.log                      # a tick, live\ncat policy/lineage.jsonl                     # the newest evolve round\ntail -3 logs/lineage.history.jsonl           # every evolve round\ntail -3 policy/gabriel_lineage.jsonl         # every language-model round\npython3 -c \"import json;print(json.load(open('evidence/evolution.json'))['lineage'][-1])\"\n```\n\nAn evolve round prints one JSON line with `wins`, `losses`, `p` and\n`accepted`.  `accepted: false` is a normal, healthy outcome -- it means the\ncandidate policy failed the sign test and the incumbent was kept.  A language\nmodel round prints `adopted` with the dev perplexity that decided it.  Rounds\nthat change nothing are the loop working, not the loop broken.\n\n## Shared hosting instead of a VPS\n\n`scripts/cron_shared.sh` is the same tick sized for a cPanel account: one\nworker, a `mkdir` lock instead of `flock`, a 5 MB log cap, an interpreter\nlookup (the system `python3` on a cPanel box is often too old -- 3.8 is the\nfloor), and **no evolve round**, because a 20-60 minute job across four cores\nis not something to run on a machine you share.  What it does run is the half\nthat accumulates across ticks anyway: harvest a few tasks, refit the language\nmodel, adopt it only if perplexity fell.  About 30-90 seconds per tick.\n\n    */15 * * * * /home/YOURUSER/astraarcengine-improved/astra/scripts/cron_shared.sh >/dev/null 2>&1\n\nEvolve rounds belong on hardware you own.  `policy/` and `evidence/` are plain\nJSON, so moving results between the two is a file copy.\n\n## Stopping\n\n```bash\nbash scripts/install_namecheap.sh --uninstall   # removes only its cron line\n```\n\nOr `crontab -e` and delete the line by hand.  To stop a round that is already\nrunning, kill it and drop the lock:\n\n```bash\npkill -f 'bench/evolve.py' ; pkill -f 'gabriel\\.' ; rm -f /tmp/astra-evolve.lock\n```\n\nNothing in `policy/` or `evidence/` is removed by uninstalling; delete those by\nhand if you genuinely want the training thrown away.\n\n## Namecheap terms\n\nWritten for a KVM VPS on a user-responsible plan.  Namecheap's own AUP and\nTerms are the authority; check them for your plan rather than trusting this\nlist.  What this workload does, so you can check it against them:\n\n* **Cron frequency.** The shared-hosting rules (one run per five minutes at\n  most, a small cap on concurrent jobs) apply to cPanel/Stellar, not to a VPS.\n  The `*/6` line stays above that five-minute floor anyway, and `flock` means\n  exactly one job is ever running.\n* **Resource use.** `nice 10`, workers capped at `min(4, nproc)` and reduced on\n  small boxes, one instance at a time.  It is a CPU-bound batch job that yields\n  to everything else on the machine.\n* **Disk.** Logs rotate at 50 MB keeping one old file; harvested evidence is\n  pruned to 40 files.  Nothing grows without a bound.\n* **Network.** After the installer's `apt-get`, the training loop makes no\n  network requests at all.  No inbound service, no open port, no proxy, no P2P,\n  no mining, no bulk mail, no scraping.\n* **Data.** Public ARC task JSON that ships in `data/arc/`.  Nothing is fetched\n  and nothing is redistributed.\n\n## Two things this is not\n\n**It is not an ARC-AGI-3 score.** ARC-AGI-3 is interactive -- an agent takes\nactions in an environment over time.  This engine is static: it reads a task,\nsynthesises a program, and returns grids.  It cannot be scored on ARC-AGI-3 at\nall, and no number produced here should be presented as one.\n\n**The numbers in `RESULTS.md` are measured on the public task files bundled in\n`data/arc/`,** not on any hidden evaluation set, and they are not official.\nTraining here shifts the engine's behaviour on the fit split of those same\npublic files; treat a change on the fit split as a hypothesis, and the holdout\nsplit -- which is never trained on and never gates anything -- as the only\nnumber worth quoting.\n"}, {"name": "README.md", "url": "/astron/xyz/README.md", "text": "# ASTRA — ARC reasoning engine, improved edition\n\nA pure Python symbolic ARC solver. It infers programs from demonstration pairs,\nvalidates each proposed rule against every demonstration, and ranks distinct\npredictions. No neural model, third-party dependencies, or retraining required.\nThe existing data and historical evidence from the supplied archive are retained.\n\n## Run inference\n\nFrom this directory, using Python 3.8 or newer:\n\n```sh\npython -m engine data/arc/arc1_6150a2bd.json --budget 20 --out predictions.json\n```\n\nAny ARC task JSON with `train` input/output pairs and `test` inputs works. Test\noutputs are optional and never passed to the solver. Output JSON contains up to\ntwo attempts per input, the selected rule, alternative hypotheses, and diagnostic\ninformation. An empty attempt object means no valid explanation was found.\nUse `--k 1` for one attempt or `--no-loo` to disable demonstration refits.\n\nThe budget is cooperative: individual Python operations can finish after their\ndeadline. Use the isolated comparison runner below when a process timeout matters.\n\nSee [`ARC-V2-V3.md`](ARC-V2-V3.md) for the version-space ranker, the typed\nprogram representation, the conjunctive cell-rule families and the cross-fitted\ntraining recipe, and [`RESULTS-V2-V3.md`](RESULTS-V2-V3.md) for what they\nmeasured.\n\n## What changed\n\n- **Compositional reasoning:** cost/depth-aware equivalence pruning, a beam\n  retaining both cheap and promising programs, and color mappings inferred from\n  demonstrations after a structural transform. Binary search has its own budget\n  share and checks output dimensions before constructing impossible grids.\n- **Evidence-based ranking:** leave-one-out evidence follows the specific rule\n  and its test predictions; incomplete refits remain neutral. Equivalent behaviors\n  cannot crowd alternatives out of voting.\n- **Relative object motion:** align objects to uniquely identified anchors,\n  recomputing movement for each grid. Shared displacement constraints resolve\n  identical copies that greedy matching mishandled.\n- **Reliable boundaries:** reject malformed grids and nonfinite scores, contain\n  generator failures, retain late inexpensive hypotheses, and expose diagnostics.\n- **Honest measurement:** an isolated before/after runner, source and task hashes,\n  fixed selection, and exact scoring of every test pair.\n\nThese are inference and architecture changes. No model training, policy update,\nor evolution run was performed. See [IMPROVEMENTS.md](IMPROVEMENTS.md) for validation\nand limitations. [RESULTS.md](RESULTS.md) contains the supplied edition's historical\n550-task results, which must not be attributed to this revision without a rerun.\n\n## Verify and compare\n\n```sh\npython -m unittest discover -s tests -v\n\n# Repeat the bundled 40-task smoke comparison on this checkout:\npython bench/regression.py --manifest evidence/regression_before.json --budget 1 --out evidence/recheck.json\npython bench/regression.py --compare evidence/regression_before.json evidence/recheck.json\n\n# Optional full public development-set evaluation (no training):\npython bench/run_arc.py --budget 20 --jobs 4 --out evidence/full_run.json\n```\n\nShort wall-clock searches vary with hardware and load. A smoke comparison tests\nregressions; it is not an official ARC score or evidence about hidden tasks.\n\n## Layout\n\n| Path | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `engine/task.py`, `engine/grid.py` | Evidence boundary and immutable grid algebra |\n| `engine/objects.py` | Cached object segmentations and features |\n| `engine/enum_core.py` | Bounded program synthesis (legacy enumerator) |\n| `engine/program.py` | Typed programs: canonical structure plus explicit parameters |\n| `engine/synthesis.py` | Typed compositional search with fitted parameter tables |\n| `engine/vspace.py` | Version-space predictive ranking (the V2 equation) |\n| `engine/library.py` | Typed macro library and its compression objective (V3) |\n| `engine/portfolio.py` | Candidate validation, refits, ranking, diagnostics |\n| `engine/solvers/` | Specialist and compositional reasoning families |\n| `engine/__main__.py` | JSON inference command |\n| `bench/regression.py` | Isolated paired evaluation, without training |\n| `tests/` | Behavioral and regression tests |\n| `data/arc/`, `evidence/` | Supplied public tasks and measured evidence |\n| `engine/learn.py`, `bench/evolve.py` | Optional learning; `--cross-fit K` runs the V3 gate |\n| `bench/conformance.py` | Python / JavaScript agreement report |\n| `bench/v2v3_report.py` | Generates `RESULTS-V2-V3.md` from evidence files |\n\nThe original architecture discussion is retained in\n[ARCHITECTURE.md](ARCHITECTURE.md), with a note distinguishing the updated design.\n\n## Current ARC results\n\nSee [`ARC-IMPROVEMENTS.md`](ARC-IMPROVEMENTS.md) for the measurements that\nshaped this revision, what was added, and the before/after numbers:\nARC-AGI-1 43.75% -> 49.25%, ARC-AGI-2 30.00% -> 33.33% on the bundled corpora.\n"}, {"name": "RESULTS-V2-V3.md", "url": "/astron/xyz/RESULTS-V2-V3.md", "text": "# Measured results: V2 ranking and V3 training\n\nEvery number on this page was produced by the code in this directory, on the task files in `data/arc/`, in this container. Nothing is estimated or carried over from another machine.\n\n## Run settings\n\n| | before | after |\n|---|---|---|\n| per-task budget | 20.0 s | 20.0 s |\n| attempts scored | top-1 and top-2 | top-1 and top-2 |\n| planner | policy/arc_planner.json | policy/arc_planner.json |\n| learned policy | none | none |\n| ranking mode | legacy (no typed candidates) | version_space_predictive |\n| solver errors | 0 | 0 |\n| CPU seconds | 7553 | 8588 |\n| wall clock (4 workers) | 1895 s | 2156 s |\n\n## Headline\n\n| corpus | tasks | before (top 1) | after (top 1) | change |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| ARC-AGI-1 | 400 | 203 (50.75%) | 226 (56.50%) | +23 |\n| ARC-AGI-2 | 150 | 51 (34.00%) | 55 (36.67%) | +4 |\n| all | 550 | 254 (46.18%) | 281 (51.09%) | +27 |\n\nARC-AGI-1 top-2: 212 -> 240 (53.00% -> 60.00%).\n\n## Paired comparison\n\nIdentical task files, identical budget, identical scorer; the comparison is per task, and the sign test is over the discordant tasks only.\n\n| | |\n|---|---:|\n| tasks compared | 550 |\n| newly solved | 32 |\n| regressed | 5 |\n| one-sided sign-test p | 3.71e-06 |\n\nNewly solved: `arc1_017c7c7b`, `arc1_10fcaaa3`, `arc1_1b60fb0c`, `arc1_2bee17df`, `arc1_31aa019c`, `arc1_3bd67248`, `arc1_3bdb4ada`, `arc1_4347f46a`, `arc1_4612dd53`, `arc1_54d82841`, `arc1_63613498`, `arc1_673ef223`, `arc1_67a423a3`, `arc1_776ffc46`, `arc1_7f4411dc`, `arc1_913fb3ed`, `arc1_91714a58`, `arc1_99fa7670`, `arc1_a61f2674`, `arc1_a65b410d`, `arc1_b230c067`, `arc1_b27ca6d3`, `arc1_c444b776`, `arc1_d0f5fe59`, `arc1_d23f8c26`, `arc1_ef135b50`, `arc1_f5b8619d`, `arc2_017c7c7b`, `arc2_10fcaaa3`, `arc2_140c817e`, `arc2_1b60fb0c`, `arc2_21f83797`\n\nRegressed: `arc1_44f52bb0`, `arc1_46f33fce`, `arc1_72ca375d`, `arc1_aabf363d`, `arc2_1d61978c`\n\n## By solver family\n\n| family | before | after | change |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| cellwise | 29 | 55 | +26 |\n| typed | 0 | 15 | +15 |\n| compose | 27 | 29 | +2 |\n| partition | 26 | 27 | +1 |\n| counting | 1 | 1 | +0 |\n| enumerate | 5 | 4 | -1 |\n| geometry | 40 | 39 | -1 |\n| tiling | 19 | 18 | -1 |\n| colormap | 3 | 2 | -1 |\n| symmetry | 13 | 11 | -2 |\n| select | 26 | 24 | -2 |\n| objects | 46 | 43 | -3 |\n| sequence | 19 | 13 | -6 |\n\n## Candidate coverage\n\nThe diagnosis that drove this pass was that most failures produced no fitting hypothesis at all, so this is the number the reach work had to move.\n\n| | before | after |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| tasks with nothing fitting the demonstrations | 212 | 134 |\n"}, {"name": "RESULTS.md", "url": "/astron/xyz/RESULTS.md", "text": "# Measured results\n\n> **Superseded.** The numbers on this page describe an earlier revision. The\n> current engine, the diagnostics that shaped it and its before/after\n> measurement are in [`ARC-IMPROVEMENTS.md`](ARC-IMPROVEMENTS.md). In short, on\n> the same 550 public task files at the same 20-second budget:\n> ARC-AGI-1 **175 -> 197 (43.75% -> 49.25%)**, ARC-AGI-2 **45 -> 50 (30.00% ->\n> 33.33%)**, 28 newly solved, 1 regressed. On the held-out split that no\n> development decision was allowed to see, 134 -> 140.\n\n\n> Historical report from the supplied archive. The numbers below describe that\n> earlier code, not the improved edition. Current revision checks and the paired\n> 40-task smoke evaluation are recorded in `IMPROVEMENTS.md` and\n> `evidence/regression_*.json`. The full 550-task benchmark has not been rerun\n> for this edition.\n\nAll numbers on this page were produced by the code in this directory, on the\n550 public ARC task files in `data/arc/`, in this environment. Nothing is\nestimated, extrapolated, or carried over from another machine.\n\n## Headline\n\n| engine | tasks solved (1 attempt) | rate | 2 attempts | rate |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| CELL4 Astra V2 (previous, Lua) | 64 / 550 | 11.6% | - | - |\n| **ASTRA** (this engine) | **209 / 550** | **38.0%** | 222 / 550 | 40.4% |\n\n**+145 tasks, +227% relative, +26.4 accuracy points.** Paired per task: **147 wins, 2 losses**, sign test p = 1.57e-41.\n\n## By corpus\n\n| corpus | tasks | previous | ASTRA | ASTRA rate |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| ARC-AGI-1 files | 400 | 52 | 168 | 42.0% |\n| ARC-AGI-2 files | 150 | 12 | 41 | 27.3% |\n\n## Run settings\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| per-task budget | 20 s wall clock |\n| attempts scored | top-1 and top-2 |\n| total CPU | 2020 s over 550 tasks (3.7 s/task mean) |\n| wall clock | 508 s at 4-way parallelism |\n| bounded solver errors | 0 (counted as unsolved) |\n| learned policy | none (stock engine) |\n\nTasks the previous engine solved and this one does not: `arc1_46f33fce`, `arc1_8eb1be9a`.\n\n\n## How the comparison is made\n\nThe previous engine (CELL4 Astra V2) is written in Lua and needs LuaJIT, which\nis not available in this container, so its column is **its own recorded\nper-task run**, shipped in `evidence/baseline-v2-arc.json` — not a re-run and\nnot an aggregate rate. The comparison is paired: identical task files,\nidentical identifiers, a per-task win/loss table, and a one-sided sign test\nover the discordant tasks. `bench/compare.py` asserts that the two task-id sets\nare identical before it counts anything.\n\n## Scoring rules\n\n- A task counts as solved only when **every** test pair of that task is exact.\n  Tasks with two test inputs must get both.\n- **top-1** is one attempt per test input. **top-2** is the two attempts\n  official ARC-AGI allows. Both are reported; top-1 is the headline.\n- Mean cell agreement is recorded per task but is **never** scored — it is a\n  diagnostic for finding near misses.\n- Solver exceptions are caught, counted, and left in the denominator as\n  unsolved. They are not excluded.\n- The solver receives train pairs and test *inputs*. Test outputs stay in the\n  harness; `engine/task.py`'s `Ctx` has no field that could hold them, and\n  `tests/test_engine.py` asserts it.\n\n## Reproducing\n\n```sh\npython3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v\npython3 bench/run_arc.py --budget 20 --jobs 4 --out evidence/run.json\npython3 bench/compare.py evidence/run.json\npython3 bench/evolve.py --rounds 3 --budget 14 --jobs 4 --final-holdout\n```\n\nTimings vary with the machine; the solved set does not, except where a task\nsits near its per-task deadline.\n\n## What the measurements say about the remaining gap\n\nTwo diagnostics were run rather than assumed, and both point the same way.\n\n**More compute buys nothing.** The identical engine measured at a 45 s per-task\nbudget solves exactly the same 209 tasks (and the same 222 at two attempts) as\nat 20 s. The search saturates well inside the budget; the bottleneck is the\nhypothesis space, not the clock.\n\n**Better ranking buys little.** `bench/headroom.py` re-runs every failed task\ncollecting *all* distinct predictions any fitting hypothesis produced, and asks\nwhether the right answer was in there and merely out-ranked. On a 70-task\nsample of the failures it was present 10 times -- about 14%. The engine is not\nmostly making the wrong choice among answers it found; on the tasks it misses\nit usually never generates the right answer at all.\n\nThat is why the last several rounds of work went into new hypothesis families\nrather than into tuning, and it is the honest statement of where the ceiling\ncurrently sits.\n\n## Honest scope\n\nThese are public development sets. Public ARC training tasks informed which\nsolver families were worth writing, and the fit split participates in policy\nselection. The holdout figure is the transfer estimate. Nothing here is an\nofficial ARC-AGI hidden-test result and it should not be read as one.\n"}, {"name": "README.md", "url": "/arc3/README.md", "text": "# ARC-AGI-3, and the language model on it\n\nARC-AGI-1 asks for a *program*: you see every demonstration at once, you write\na rule, you are graded on a held-out grid. ARC-AGI-3 asks for a *policy*: you\nsee one frame, you choose one action, and the only feedback is the next frame\nand a counter that occasionally says a level was completed. Nothing about the\nsymbolic engine in `astron/xyz/engine/` transfers directly, because there is no\ninput-output pair to explain.\n\nWhat does transfer is GABRIEL.\n\n## The connection\n\n`astron/xyz/gabriel/lm.py` is a sparse log-linear model over tokens conditioned\non a **signature** — a short list of discrete strings describing the thing being\nreasoned about. For ARC-1 the signature describes a task and the token is a\nsolver family or an operator. Nothing in the model is specific to programs, so\nthe same class, the same feature map, the same optimiser and the same\nconstrained decode answer a different question here:\n\n| | ARC-AGI-1 | ARC-AGI-3 |\n|---|---|---|\n| signature | task signatures | frame signature (`arc3/signature.py`) |\n| token | program / operator | action (`arc3/actions.py`) |\n| corpus | fitted programs from `evidence/*.json` | journals the agent wrote (`arc3/corpus.py`) |\n| decode | `beam_chains`, constrained to real operators | `dist`, constrained to legal moves |\n| status | a proposal distribution, never evidence | the same |\n\nNo line of `gabriel/lm.py` changed.\n\n## Two things make it worth doing\n\n**The signature is symbolic, not pixels.** A model conditioned on raw frames\nwould have to see a frame twice to say anything, and in ARC-3 it never does —\nthe games are generated and the boards are large. Conditioned on *\"twelve\ncomponents, the largest covers a third of the board, the last action changed\nnothing\"*, it has something to say about a board nobody has played.\n\n**An action token names a target, not a coordinate.** Clicking cell (17, 4) is\nnot a fact that transfers; clicking *the smallest component, which is red* is.\nSo `a|6@c4/s2/r0` is the token and the coordinate is recovered from the current\nframe at decision time. This is the move the cell tree's copy leaves make —\nname the thing, not the value — and it is the only reason a model trained on\nsome games can be worth anything on the next one.\n\n## What the model may and may not do\n\nThe base explorer decides which moves are legal and whether a move is worth\ntrying. When several are equally untried it breaks the tie with a fixed\nheuristic, and **that heuristic is the only thing the model replaces**.\nEverything else runs first and wins: a level change clears the graph, a\n`GAME_OVER` resets, an untried action beats a tried one, the frontier search\nplans over observed transitions, and a transition seen to be nondeterministic\nstays unusable. If the model is untrained, indifferent or wrong, the worst it\ncan do is reorder moves the explorer was going to try anyway — and\n`test_it_matches_the_plain_explorer_when_untrained` holds it to that.\n\n## Credit assignment\n\nA weight of `2**-E` for an energy read off the outcome, the same discipline the\nV3 soft verified targets use:\n\n| | E | weight | why |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| on the path to a completed level | 0 | 1 | which action in the window mattered is exactly what is not observable |\n| changed the frame | 2 | ¼ | weak, and nearly the only evidence there is |\n| changed nothing | 6 | 1/64 | *\"left does nothing on a board like this\"* is real, and must not drown the rest |\n\n## Held out by game, not by step\n\nSteps inside one episode are overwhelmingly similar — the board barely changes\nbetween consecutive actions — so a step-level split measures memorisation of an\nepisode and calls it generalisation. `arc3/train.py` splits by **game**, which\nasks the question that matters: does what this model learned on some games say\nanything about a game it has never played?\n\n## Four agents, so the two ideas can be told apart\n\n| agent | state key | order of untried moves |\n|---|---|---|\n| `explorer` | exact frame | fixed heuristic |\n| `volatile` | frame minus self-moving cells | fixed heuristic |\n| `gabriel` | exact frame | learned |\n| `volatile-gabriel` | frame minus self-moving cells | learned |\n\nThe abstraction exists because exact frame hashing is fatal in a way that does\nnot announce itself: in any game with a timer, a score readout or an idle\nanimation every frame is unique, so every state is new, so there is always an\nuntried action and the planner is never reached. A cell is disowned when it\nchanged on almost every step *whatever action was taken* — a severe test, since\na cell that matters changes when you act on it and not otherwise. Over-merging\nis guarded rather than assumed away: the explorer already marks a transition\nseen to lead to two successors as unusable, so a bad abstraction costs planning\nrather than corrupting it, and the abstraction is refused outright unless most\nof the board survives it.\n\n## The volatility abstraction does not fire on these games\n\nI built `volatile` on a hypothesis and the hypothesis is wrong. Measured\ndirectly — per-cell change rates over 299 steps on six games:\n\n| game | cells | changed >90% of steps | >50% | >20% |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| ar25 | 4096 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| bp35 | 4096 | 0 | 0 | 111 |\n| cd82 | 4096 | 0 | 0 | 2 |\n| cn04 | 4096 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| dc22 | 4096 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| ft09 | 4096 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n\n**No cell in any of these games changes on more than half the steps.** There is\nno timer, no score readout, no idle animation for the abstraction to disown, and\n`volatile` is byte-identical to `explorer` across all 25 games — same 2 levels,\nsame 8585 distinct states, same 0.2107 no-op rate. The graph closes on its own:\n8585 distinct states over 14594 steps means two steps in five revisit a state.\n\nThe threshold is not lowered to force it. At 20% a moving character's own cells\nwould qualify and the abstraction would destroy exactly the state it exists to\npreserve. The agent stays, guarded and tested, because the mechanism is right\nfor a game that *does* have a counter — but on these environments it is a\nmeasured null and is not claimed as anything else.\n\n## What is measured, and what is only a proxy\n\n`levels_completed` is the metric that matters and it is desperately sparse:\n**two** in fifteen thousand steps across all 25 games. Moving it reliably at\nthis budget is not something a single change can be expected to do, so two\ndense statistics are recorded alongside it — distinct states reached, and\nno-op rate. They are what an explorer is actually doing between level\ncompletions. They are proxies and are labelled as such; a run that improves\nthem and not the level count has improved a proxy.\n\n## Running it\n\n```sh\n# the state-abstraction ablation, no model involved\npython3 arc3/run_offline.py --agent explorer --actions 600 --seed 0 --out A.json\npython3 arc3/run_offline.py --agent volatile --actions 600 --seed 0 --out B.json\n\n# collect journals with the untrained agent (it behaves exactly as the explorer)\npython3 arc3/run_offline.py --agent gabriel --journal --actions 600 --seed 0 \\\n    --out astron/xyz/evidence/arc3_collect_s0.json\n\n# train, holding out whole games\npython3 arc3/train.py --journals astron/xyz/evidence/arc3_collect_s0.json \\\n    --out astron/xyz/policy/arc3_lm.json --report astron/xyz/evidence/arc3_train.json\n\n# measure against the unguided explorer on identical games and budgets\npython3 arc3/run_offline.py --agent explorer --actions 1000 --seed 0 --out A.json\npython3 arc3/run_offline.py --agent gabriel  --actions 1000 --seed 0 --out B.json\n```\n\n`python3 -m unittest arc3.test_gabriel` covers the guards.\n\n## Scope\n\nThese are the **local public development environments**, run offline through the\nofficial 0.9.9 SDK with sockets blocked before the SDK is constructed\n(`network_attempts` is reported in every record and is 0). This is not an\nofficial competition score and nothing here claims one. There is no external\nmodel, no API key and no network at any point; the weights start at zero and\nare trained from scratch on journals the agent wrote itself.\n"}];
